// Authored variants: the same fact asked from a different angle (application, scenario, prediction, NOT/EXCEPT)
const QB_VARIANTS_1 = [
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which branched-chain amino acid is purely ketogenic?', kind:'scenario', q:'A researcher gives hepatocytes a uniformly ¹⁴C-labeled branched-chain amino acid and finds label in acetoacetate and 3-hydroxybutyrate but never in newly made glucose. Which amino acid was supplied?', choices:['Leucine','Valine','Isoleucine','Alanine'], ans:0, exp:'**Leucine** is purely ketogenic: its skeleton is degraded only to acetyl-CoA and acetoacetate, which cannot give net glucose. Isoleucine is both glucogenic and ketogenic (it also yields succinyl-CoA), and valine is purely glucogenic via succinyl-CoA. Mnemonic: the only purely ketogenic amino acids are the two L\'s, **leucine and lysine**.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which amino acid has a rigid cyclic pyrrolidine ring that disrupts α-helices and is frequently found at β-turns?', kind:'prediction', q:'A protein engineer replaces a residue in the middle of a long α-helix with proline. What is the most likely structural consequence?', choices:['The helix is kinked or broken at that position','The helix is stabilized by a new hydrogen bond','The helix is converted into a parallel β-sheet','The helix is unchanged because the side chain points outward'], ans:0, exp:'Proline\'s side chain bonds back to its own backbone nitrogen, so the **rigid ring** fixes the backbone angle and leaves no N–H to donate a helical hydrogen bond. A mid-helix proline therefore kinks or breaks the α-helix, which is why proline is common at β-turns and helix ends rather than inside helices.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which aromatic amino acid is the precursor to tyrosine and accumulates in phenylketonuria (PKU)?', kind:'scenario', q:'A newborn is diagnosed with phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency and placed on a phenylalanine-restricted diet. Which amino acid becomes essential and must now be supplied in the diet?', choices:['Tyrosine','Phenylalanine','Tryptophan','Histidine'], ans:0, exp:'Phenylalanine hydroxylase normally converts **phenylalanine into tyrosine**; in PKU phenylalanine accumulates (so it is restricted) and tyrosine can no longer be made, so it becomes **conditionally essential**. Phenylalanine is always essential, so it is the amino acid being limited, not the newly required one.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which polar amino acid has a hydroxymethyl side chain, is the most commonly phosphorylated residue, and is the nucleophile in serine proteases?', kind:'application', q:'Organophosphate nerve agents irreversibly inactivate acetylcholinesterase by covalently modifying the hydroxyl nucleophile of its catalytic triad. Which residue do they modify?', choices:['Serine','Cysteine','Threonine','Tyrosine'], ans:0, exp:'Acetylcholinesterase, like chymotrypsin, is a **serine hydrolase**: a His in the catalytic triad activates the serine –CH₂OH to attack the substrate. Organophosphates phosphorylate that serine, permanently blocking the enzyme. Cysteine proteases use a thiol nucleophile instead.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which amino acid is the amide derivative of aspartate, is polar and uncharged, and is the attachment site for N-linked glycosylation?', kind:'application', q:'Tunicamycin blocks the transfer of a core oligosaccharide onto the side-chain amide nitrogen of residues in proteins entering the rough ER. Which residue normally receives this sugar?', choices:['Asparagine','Serine','Glutamine','Threonine'], ans:0, exp:'**N-linked glycosylation** attaches the oligosaccharide to the amide nitrogen of **asparagine** (in an Asn-X-Ser/Thr sequon) cotranslationally in the rough ER. Serine and threonine carry **O-linked** sugars, added mainly in the Golgi; glutamine also has an amide but is not used for N-glycosylation.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which positively charged amino acid has an ε-amino group (pKa about 10.5), is commonly acetylated on histones, and is purely ketogenic?', kind:'prediction', q:'Histone acetyltransferases add acetyl groups to the ε-amino groups of lysines on histone tails. What is the direct effect of this modification?', choices:['Their positive charge is removed, loosening DNA binding','They gain a negative charge that tightens DNA binding','They become more hydrophobic and form heterochromatin','They gain a positive charge that compacts nucleosomes'], ans:0, exp:'Lysine\'s ε-amino group (pKa ≈ 10.5) is protonated and positive at physiological pH, so it binds the negative DNA backbone. **Acetylation** turns it into a neutral amide, weakening histone–DNA attraction and opening chromatin (euchromatin) for transcription; deacetylation does the reverse.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which amino acid has an imidazole side chain with a pKa near 6, can donate or accept protons at physiological pH, and is found in enzyme catalytic triads?', kind:'prediction', q:'An enzyme loses activity when the pH rises above about 6.5 because one active-site residue can no longer donate a proton. Which residue is most likely responsible?', choices:['Histidine','Lysine','Arginine','Aspartate'], ans:0, exp:'**Histidine**\'s imidazole has a pKa near 6, so small pH changes around neutrality switch it between protonated (donor) and neutral (acceptor) forms, which makes it ideal for general acid–base catalysis. Lysine (≈10.5) and arginine (≈12.5) stay protonated at pH 6.5, and aspartate (≈3.9) is already deprotonated.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Which amino acid donates the second nitrogen of urea by condensing with citrulline to form argininosuccinate?', kind:'application', q:'In the urea cycle, argininosuccinate lyase releases fumarate, which can enter the citric acid cycle. The carbon skeleton of which amino acid leaves the urea cycle as this fumarate?', choices:['Aspartate','Glutamate','Ornithine','Glutamine'], ans:0, exp:'**Aspartate** condenses with citrulline to form argininosuccinate, donating its amino nitrogen (the second N of urea); lyase then splits off its four-carbon skeleton as **fumarate**. This aspartate–argininosuccinate shunt links the urea and citric acid cycles. Ornithine is the carrier regenerated each turn, not the nitrogen donor.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'At a very low pH, amino acids are _____', kind:'prediction', q:'Alanine is dissolved in a buffer at pH 1 and placed in an electric field. How does it migrate?', choices:['Toward the cathode, with a net +1 charge','Toward the anode, with a net −1 charge','It stays put, as a neutral zwitterion','Toward the cathode, with a net +2 charge'], ans:0, exp:'At pH 1, far below both pKa values, alanine is **fully protonated**: –COOH (neutral) and –NH₃⁺ (positive), for a net +1 charge, so it moves toward the negative **cathode**. A +2 charge is impossible because alanine has only one basic group.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'At the half-equivalence point (midpoint of a buffering region) in a titration, pH = _____', kind:'application', q:'During titration of glycine with NaOH, the pH reads 2.34, equal to the pKa of its α-carboxyl group. Which relationship describes that group at this point?', choices:['[COO⁻] = [COOH]','[COO⁻] = 10 [COOH]','[COOH] = 10 [COO⁻]','[COO⁻] = 2 [COOH]'], ans:0, exp:'By Henderson–Hasselbalch, pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]); when **pH = pKa** the log term is 0, so the acid is exactly half deprotonated. This midpoint of the buffering plateau is where the solution resists pH change best; a tenfold ratio corresponds to pH one unit away from the pKa.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'For an amino acid with a neutral side chain titrated from low pH, the pH at the first equivalence point equals the _____', kind:'prediction', q:'Valine (pKa 2.32 and 9.62) is titrated from pH 1 with NaOH. After exactly one equivalent of base has been added, which species predominates?', choices:['The zwitterion, with no net charge','The cation, with a net +1 charge','The anion, with a net −1 charge','Equal amounts of cation and zwitterion'], ans:0, exp:'One equivalent of base removes all the carboxyl protons, leaving the **zwitterion**; this first equivalence point is the **pI**, (2.32 + 9.62)/2 = 5.97. Equal amounts of cation and zwitterion exist at the half-equivalence point, where pH = pKa₁.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Glycine has pKa values of 2.34 (α-carboxyl) and 9.60 (α-amino) and no ionizable side chain. What is its isoelectric point?', kind:'application', q:'Glycine (pKa 2.34 and 9.60) is loaded onto an isoelectric focusing gel with a pH gradient from 3 to 10. At about what pH does it stop migrating?', choices:['5.97','7.00','4.80','9.60'], ans:0, exp:'In isoelectric focusing a molecule stops where its net charge is zero, its **pI**. For an amino acid with no ionizable side chain, pI = (pKa₁ + pKa₂)/2 = (2.34 + 9.60)/2 = **5.97**. Neutral pH (7.00) is not the pI, and 9.60 is just the amino pKa.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Lysine has pKa values of 2.18 (α-carboxyl), 8.95 (α-amino) and 10.53 (side-chain amino). What is its isoelectric point?', kind:'prediction', q:'Lysine (pKa 2.18, 8.95 and 10.53) is placed in a buffer at pH 7.4 in an electric field. How does it migrate?', choices:['Toward the cathode, because the pH is below its pI','Toward the anode, because the pH is below its pI','Toward the anode, because the pH is above its pI','It does not move, because the pH equals its pI'], ans:0, exp:'For a basic amino acid, pI is the average of the two amino pKa values: (8.95 + 10.53)/2 = **9.74**. At pH 7.4, below the pI, lysine carries a net positive charge and moves toward the negative **cathode**. Rule: pH < pI means positive; pH > pI means negative.'},
  {ofTag:'Amino Acids', of:'Aspartate has pKa values of 1.88 (α-carboxyl), 3.65 (side-chain carboxyl) and 9.60 (α-amino). What is its isoelectric point?', kind:'application', q:'A solution of aspartate (pKa 1.88, 3.65 and 9.60) is adjusted to pH 2.77. What is the predominant net charge on aspartate?', choices:['0','+1','−1','−2'], ans:0, exp:'For an acidic amino acid, pI is the average of the two pKa values that flank the neutral species, the two carboxyls: (1.88 + 3.65)/2 = **2.77**. At its pI aspartate is mainly the neutral zwitterion. Averaging 3.65 and 9.60 is the common mistake; at physiological pH aspartate is about −1.'},
  {ofTag:'Proteins', of:'The 3° structure of proteins is driven primarily by _____', kind:'prediction', q:'In a soluble globular protein, a buried leucine in the core is mutated to aspartate. Why does this most likely destabilize the folded state?', choices:['It buries a charge in the core and weakens the hydrophobic effect','It breaks a peptide bond in the polypeptide backbone','It removes a disulfide bond that held the core together','It blocks α-helix hydrogen bonding along the whole chain'], ans:0, exp:'Tertiary folding is driven mainly by the **hydrophobic effect**: nonpolar side chains are buried so that water gains entropy. Placing a charged aspartate in the core costs a large desolvation penalty and disrupts packing. A substitution cannot break the backbone peptide bond, and leucine forms no disulfides.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes', of:'Enzymes _____ the ΔG or ΔH of a reaction, or its final equilibrium position', kind:'except', q:'An enzyme is added to a reaction with ΔG°′ = −20 kJ/mol. All of the following change EXCEPT:', choices:['The equilibrium constant','The activation energy','The forward reaction rate','The time needed to reach equilibrium'], ans:0, exp:'Enzymes lower the **activation energy** for both directions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster, but ΔG, ΔH and **Keq** are state functions of reactants and products and do not change. Because both forward and reverse rates rise by the same factor, the ratio at equilibrium is unchanged.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Kinetics', of:'The substrate concentration at which an enzyme reaches half of its maximum velocity is the _____', kind:'application', q:'Two isozymes act on the same substrate: isozyme A has Km = 0.1 mM and isozyme B has Km = 10 mM. Which statement is correct?', choices:['Isozyme A reaches half-maximal velocity at a lower [S]','Isozyme B has the higher apparent affinity for substrate','Isozyme A must have the higher Vmax of the two','Isozyme B is saturated at a lower substrate concentration'], ans:0, exp:'**Km** is the [S] at which v₀ = ½Vmax, so a low Km means half-saturation at low [S] and high apparent affinity. Km says nothing about Vmax. Hexokinase (low Km) versus glucokinase (high Km) is the classic MCAT example.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Kinetics', of:'The reaction velocity reached when all of an enzyme\'s active sites are saturated with substrate is the _____', kind:'prediction', q:'In a Michaelis–Menten assay with substrate in large excess, the enzyme concentration is doubled. What happens to Vmax and Km?', choices:['Vmax doubles; Km is unchanged','Vmax is unchanged; Km halves','Vmax and Km both double','Vmax doubles; Km halves'], ans:0, exp:'**Vmax = kcat[E]total**: it is the rate when every active site is saturated, so it scales with the amount of enzyme. Km is a property of each enzyme–substrate pair and does not depend on enzyme concentration.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Kinetics', of:'Multisubunit enzymes that bind substrate cooperatively display a _____ plot of velocity versus substrate concentration', kind:'application', q:'An enzyme assay gives an S-shaped plot of v₀ versus [S], and the calculated Hill coefficient is 2.8. What does this most likely indicate?', choices:['Positive cooperativity among several subunits','Competitive inhibition by accumulating product','A single active site obeying Michaelis–Menten kinetics','Negative cooperativity among several subunits'], ans:0, exp:'A **sigmoidal** curve with a Hill coefficient greater than 1 means **positive cooperativity**: substrate binding at one subunit raises the affinity of the others. A single-site Michaelis–Menten enzyme gives a hyperbola (Hill coefficient 1), and negative cooperativity gives a coefficient below 1.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Kinetics', of:'Which expression is the Michaelis-Menten equation for the initial reaction velocity v₀?', kind:'application', q:'An enzyme has Km = 4 μM and Vmax = 100 μmol/min. What is the initial velocity when [S] = 12 μM?', choices:['75 μmol/min','25 μmol/min','33 μmol/min','50 μmol/min'], ans:0, exp:'**v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S])** = 100 × 12/(4 + 12) = 100 × 0.75 = 75 μmol/min. A useful check: at [S] = Km, v₀ = ½Vmax, and at [S] = 3Km, v₀ = ¾Vmax.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Regulation', of:'_____ occurs when the end product of a metabolic pathway inhibits an enzyme that acts earlier in the same pathway', kind:'scenario', q:'In a bacterial culture, adding excess isoleucine quickly halts isoleucine synthesis because isoleucine binds a regulatory site on threonine deaminase, the first enzyme of its pathway. What kind of regulation is this?', choices:['Feedback inhibition','Feed-forward activation','Competitive inhibition','Zymogen activation'], ans:0, exp:'When a pathway\'s **end product** inhibits an early, usually committed enzyme, it is **feedback inhibition**, which prevents wasteful overproduction. Isoleucine binds an allosteric site rather than the active site, so this is not competitive inhibition; feed-forward activation runs in the other direction (an early metabolite activates a later enzyme).'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Regulation', of:'An inhibitor that binds the active site of free enzyme and thus prevents substrate from binding is a _____', kind:'scenario', q:'Fomepizole or ethanol is given in methanol poisoning because it occupies the active site of alcohol dehydrogenase. How does this change the kinetics of methanol oxidation?', choices:['Apparent Km rises; Vmax is unchanged','Apparent Km is unchanged; Vmax falls','Apparent Km and Vmax both fall','Apparent Km falls; Vmax rises'], ans:0, exp:'A **competitive inhibitor** binds only the free enzyme\'s active site, so enough substrate can outcompete it: Vmax is unchanged, but more substrate is needed for half-maximal velocity (**apparent Km rises**). Slowing methanol oxidation lets it be excreted before it forms toxic formaldehyde and formic acid.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Regulation', of:'An inhibitor that binds only to the enzyme-substrate complex, not to free enzyme, is a _____', kind:'application', q:'An inhibitor lowers both apparent Km and Vmax by the same factor, giving a Lineweaver–Burk line parallel to the uninhibited one. Where does this inhibitor bind?', choices:['Only to the enzyme–substrate complex','Only to the active site of free enzyme','Equally to free enzyme and the ES complex','Covalently to the active-site nucleophile'], ans:0, exp:'An **uncompetitive** inhibitor binds only ES, locking substrate in (Km falls) while preventing catalysis (Vmax falls) by the same factor, hence **parallel lines**. Binding free enzyme only is competitive (Km up), and binding both equally is noncompetitive (Vmax down, Km unchanged).'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Regulation', of:'An inhibitor that binds an allosteric site with equal affinity for free enzyme and the enzyme-substrate complex is a _____', kind:'prediction', q:'A pure noncompetitive inhibitor is added to an enzyme assay. How do Vmax and Km change?', choices:['Vmax falls; Km is unchanged','Vmax is unchanged; Km rises','Vmax and Km both fall','Vmax falls; Km rises'], ans:0, exp:'A **noncompetitive** inhibitor binds an allosteric site equally well on E and ES, so it removes functional enzyme (**Vmax falls**) without changing substrate affinity (**Km unchanged**). Adding more substrate cannot overcome it. Unequal binding to E and ES would be mixed inhibition, which changes Km as well.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Regulation', of:'On a Lineweaver-Burk plot, an inhibitor that leaves the y-intercept unchanged but makes the slope steeper (moving the x-intercept toward the origin) is a _____', kind:'except', q:'A Lineweaver–Burk plot is made with and without a competitive inhibitor. All of the following are true of the inhibited line EXCEPT:', choices:['Its y-intercept is higher','Its slope is steeper','Its x-intercept lies closer to the origin','It meets the uninhibited line on the y-axis'], ans:0, exp:'The y-intercept is **1/Vmax**, and competitive inhibitors do not change Vmax, so the lines meet on the y-axis. The slope (Km/Vmax) increases and the x-intercept (−1/Km) moves toward zero because the apparent Km rises.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Regulation', of:'An irreversible inhibitor typically _____ the enzyme, often at its active site, permanently eliminating its activity', kind:'application', q:'Aspirin acetylates a serine in the active site of platelet cyclooxygenase, and COX activity returns only as new platelets are made. Which description fits aspirin?', choices:['An irreversible inhibitor that covalently modifies the enzyme','A competitive inhibitor that excess substrate can displace','An uncompetitive inhibitor that binds only the ES complex','A noncompetitive inhibitor that binds reversibly elsewhere'], ans:0, exp:'Aspirin **covalently** modifies COX, so activity can be restored only by making new enzyme. Platelets lack nuclei and cannot make new COX, so the effect lasts for the platelet\'s lifespan (about 7–10 days). Reversible inhibitors, competitive or not, dissociate when the drug is cleared.'},
  {ofTag:'Enzymes: Regulation', of:'_____ is irreversible inhibition in which an enzyme converts a substrate analog into a reactive intermediate that covalently inactivates the enzyme itself', kind:'scenario', q:'5-Fluorouracil is converted to FdUMP, which thymidylate synthase begins to process as if it were dUMP; the reaction stalls with the enzyme trapped in a covalent complex. What type of inhibition is this?', choices:['Suicide inhibition','Competitive inhibition','Uncompetitive inhibition','Feedback inhibition'], ans:0, exp:'In **suicide (mechanism-based) inhibition**, the enzyme\'s own catalytic mechanism turns a substrate analog into a species that bonds covalently to the enzyme, inactivating it irreversibly. A simple competitive inhibitor would bind reversibly and could be outcompeted by dUMP.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Replication', of:'_____ relieves the positive supercoiling (torsional strain) that builds up ahead of the replication fork by nicking and resealing the DNA backbone', kind:'application', q:'Fluoroquinolone antibiotics inhibit bacterial DNA gyrase, a type II topoisomerase. What is the most direct consequence for DNA replication?', choices:['Positive supercoils accumulate ahead of the fork and stall it','Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand cannot be joined','RNA primers cannot be laid down on the lagging strand','Hydrogen bonds at the origin cannot be broken'], ans:0, exp:'Unwinding at the fork overwinds the DNA ahead of it; **topoisomerases** (gyrase in bacteria) cut and reseal the backbone to remove these positive supercoils. Without them torsional strain builds until the fork stalls. Breaking base-pair hydrogen bonds is helicase\'s job, and joining fragments is ligase\'s.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Replication', of:'_____ breaks the hydrogen bonds between base pairs to separate the two DNA strands at the replication fork', kind:'prediction', q:'A temperature-sensitive bacterial mutant stops DNA synthesis at the restrictive temperature, and its origins never become single-stranded even though topoisomerase is active. Which enzyme is most likely defective?', choices:['Helicase','Primase','DNA ligase','DNA polymerase III'], ans:0, exp:'**Helicase** uses ATP to break the hydrogen bonds between base pairs and open the double helix; without it no single-stranded template ever forms. Primase and DNA polymerase need that single-stranded template to work, and ligase acts only after fragments are made.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Replication', of:'_____ are short, newly synthesized DNA segments formed discontinuously on the lagging strand during DNA replication', kind:'scenario', q:'Bacteria are pulse-labeled with radioactive thymidine for a few seconds, and their DNA is denatured; many short labeled pieces of about 1,000–2,000 nucleotides are found. Why do these pieces form?', choices:['The lagging strand is made discontinuously, away from the fork','The leading strand is made in short bursts from many origins','Exonucleases cut the new DNA into fragments after synthesis','Primase makes long RNA segments that are later degraded'], ans:0, exp:'DNA polymerase works only 5′→3′, so the strand whose template runs the other way is built in short pieces, **Okazaki fragments**, each started by a new primer as the fork opens. The leading strand is made continuously from a single primer.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Replication', of:'_____ seals nicks between adjacent DNA fragments, such as Okazaki fragments, by forming phosphodiester bonds', kind:'prediction', q:'In a bacterial strain with a temperature-sensitive DNA ligase, what accumulates during replication at the restrictive temperature?', choices:['Short unjoined fragments of lagging-strand DNA','Long leading strands still capped by RNA primers','Positive supercoils ahead of each replication fork','Single-stranded template coated with SSB protein'], ans:0, exp:'**DNA ligase** forms the final phosphodiester bond between adjacent Okazaki fragments after the primers are replaced with DNA. Without it the lagging strand remains a series of nicked, unjoined pieces. Supercoils reflect topoisomerase failure, not ligase failure.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Repair', of:'_____ proofreads its own work during replication and excises incorrectly paired bases', kind:'prediction', q:'A mutant DNA polymerase lacks its 3′→5′ exonuclease activity but polymerizes normally. What is the expected effect?', choices:['The mutation rate rises because mispaired bases are not removed','Replication stops because RNA primers cannot be removed','Okazaki fragments can no longer be joined together','The mutation rate falls because fewer bases are excised'], ans:0, exp:'The **3′→5′ exonuclease** is DNA polymerase\'s proofreading activity: it backs up and removes a just-added mismatched nucleotide. Losing it raises the error rate roughly 100-fold. Primer removal uses the separate 5′→3′ exonuclease (Pol I in bacteria), and joining fragments is ligase\'s role.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Repair', of:'According to standard MCAT review sources, mismatch repair of DNA using the genes MSH2 and MLH1 occurs during _____', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient with Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer) carries a loss-of-function mutation in MSH2. Which type of DNA error most likely persists in this patient\'s cells?', choices:['Base mismatches left uncorrected after replication','Thymine dimers caused by ultraviolet light','Uracils formed by cytosine deamination','Double-strand breaks from ionizing radiation'], ans:0, exp:'**MSH2 and MLH1** run **mismatch repair**, which fixes mispaired bases and small loops missed by polymerase proofreading after S phase (MCAT sources place it in G2). Defects cause microsatellite instability and Lynch syndrome. Thymine dimers need nucleotide excision repair, and deaminated cytosines need base excision repair.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Repair', of:'_____ fixes helix-deforming lesions of DNA, such as thymine dimers', kind:'scenario', q:'A child with xeroderma pigmentosum develops multiple skin cancers after minimal sun exposure. Which DNA repair pathway is defective?', choices:['Nucleotide excision repair','Base excision repair','Mismatch repair','Nonhomologous end joining'], ans:0, exp:'UV light creates **thymine dimers**, bulky helix-distorting lesions that are removed by **nucleotide excision repair**, which cuts out an oligonucleotide around the damage. Xeroderma pigmentosum results from defects in NER genes. Base excision repair handles small, non-distorting base lesions.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Repair', of:'_____ fixes small, non-helix-deforming lesions such as deaminated cytosine by removing the damaged base, leaving an apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) site', kind:'prediction', q:'Spontaneous deamination converts a cytosine in DNA into uracil. Which process repairs this lesion?', choices:['Base excision repair, starting with a glycosylase','Nucleotide excision repair of a bulky segment','Mismatch repair guided by strand methylation','Homologous recombination using a sister chromatid'], ans:0, exp:'In **base excision repair**, uracil-DNA glycosylase removes the single wrong base, leaving an AP site that an endonuclease, polymerase and ligase then fill. Using thymine rather than uracil in DNA is what lets cells recognize deaminated cytosine as damage. NER is reserved for bulky, helix-distorting lesions.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Repair', of:'_____ code for proteins that slow cell cycling or promote DNA repair', kind:'scenario', q:'A child inherits one mutant RB1 allele, and retinoblastoma develops only after the remaining normal allele is lost in a retinal cell. What does this pattern indicate about RB1?', choices:['It is a tumor suppressor gene','It is a proto-oncogene','It is a dominant oncogene','It is a housekeeping gene'], ans:0, exp:'**Tumor suppressor genes** such as RB1 and TP53 restrain the cell cycle, so both copies must be lost before control fails (Knudson **two-hit** hypothesis). Oncogenes act dominantly: one gain-of-function allele is enough.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Chromosomes', of:'_____ are repetitive TTAGGG sequences at the ends of linear chromosomes that protect coding DNA from being lost during replication and can be extended by a reverse transcriptase enzyme', kind:'prediction', q:'Human somatic cells that lack telomerase are grown in culture for many divisions. What happens to their chromosomes?', choices:['Their ends shorten with each round of replication','Their centromeres lengthen with each division','Their ends lengthen with each round of replication','Their origins of replication are lost first'], ans:0, exp:'The lagging strand cannot be completed at the very end of a linear chromosome, so **telomeres** shorten with every division; telomerase (a reverse transcriptase carrying an RNA template) extends them. When telomeres become critically short, cells senesce. Cancer cells often reactivate telomerase to escape this limit.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Translation', of:'The _____ of a codon can pair with the tRNA anticodon by non-Watson–Crick wobble pairing, usually without changing the amino acid', kind:'application', q:'Cells have fewer than 61 different tRNA anticodons yet decode all 61 sense codons. What explains this?', choices:['Relaxed base pairing at the third codon position','Relaxed base pairing at the first codon position','Some tRNAs carry two different amino acids','Ribosomes ignore the second base of each codon'], ans:0, exp:'**Wobble** pairing at the codon\'s **third (3′) base** lets one anticodon, often containing inosine, read several codons for the same amino acid. This is why most synonymous codons differ only in the third position, and why mutations there are often silent.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Translation', of:'A _____ converts a codon for an amino acid into a premature stop codon', kind:'prediction', q:'A point mutation changes a tryptophan codon (UGG) to UGA in the middle of a gene. What is the most likely effect on the protein?', choices:['It is truncated at that position','It has a single amino acid substituted','It is unchanged in sequence','It has every downstream residue altered'], ans:0, exp:'UGA is a stop codon, so this is a **nonsense mutation**: translation terminates early, producing a truncated protein (and the mRNA is often destroyed by nonsense-mediated decay). Changing every downstream residue describes a frameshift, and a single swap describes a missense mutation.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Translation', of:'A _____ changes a codon so that it specifies a different amino acid', kind:'scenario', q:'In sickle cell disease, an A→T change converts codon 6 of β-globin from GAG (glutamate) to GTG (valine). What kind of mutation is this?', choices:['Missense mutation','Nonsense mutation','Silent mutation','Frameshift mutation'], ans:0, exp:'A single-base substitution that changes the encoded amino acid is a **missense mutation**; here a charged Glu becomes a hydrophobic Val, causing HbS to polymerize when deoxygenated. It is not silent (the amino acid changes), not nonsense (no stop codon), and not a frameshift (no insertion or deletion).'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Translation', of:'A _____ results from inserting or deleting a number of nucleotides that is not a multiple of three, which changes every downstream codon', kind:'except', q:'Which of the following changes in a coding exon would NOT cause a frameshift?', choices:['Deletion of 3 nucleotides','Insertion of 1 nucleotide','Deletion of 2 nucleotides','Insertion of 4 nucleotides'], ans:0, exp:'Codons are read in triplets, so only insertions or deletions that are **not multiples of three** shift the reading frame. Deleting 3 nucleotides removes one codon (an in-frame deletion, as in the common ΔF508 cystic fibrosis allele) and leaves downstream codons intact.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Translation', of:'The 5\' cap and poly-A tail of eukaryotic mRNA mainly _____', kind:'prediction', q:'A eukaryotic mRNA is injected into the cytoplasm without a 5′ cap or a poly-A tail. Compared with normally processed mRNA, what is expected?', choices:['It is degraded faster and translated less','It is degraded slower and translated more','It is held in the nucleus for further splicing','It is translated normally but cannot leave the ER'], ans:0, exp:'The **5′ cap** and **poly-A tail** protect mRNA from exonucleases and help recruit the ribosome (the cap is recognized by initiation factors and the tail by poly-A binding protein). An uncapped, untailed mRNA is therefore rapidly degraded and poorly translated.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA: Translation', of:'In prokaryotic translation initiation, the 30S ribosomal subunit binds the _____, a purine-rich sequence just upstream of the start codon', kind:'application', q:'In a bacterial gene, the purine-rich sequence about 8 nucleotides upstream of the AUG start codon is deleted. Which step is most directly impaired?', choices:['Binding of the 30S subunit to the mRNA','Binding of RNA polymerase to the promoter','Release of the polypeptide at the stop codon','Attachment of methionine to the initiator tRNA'], ans:0, exp:'The **Shine–Dalgarno** sequence base-pairs with 16S rRNA in the 30S subunit, positioning the start codon in the P site. Without it, translation initiation fails even though transcription is normal. Eukaryotes instead use the 5′ cap and scanning.'},
  {ofTag:'General', of:'During translation elongation, each incoming aminoacyl-tRNA first enters the ribosome at the _____', kind:'prediction', q:'Tetracycline blocks the ribosomal site where incoming aminoacyl-tRNAs bind. Which step of translation does it inhibit?', choices:['Delivery of aminoacyl-tRNAs during elongation','Exit of uncharged tRNAs from the ribosome','Recognition of the start codon at initiation','Assembly of the large subunit at initiation'], ans:0, exp:'Each new aminoacyl-tRNA enters at the **A (aminoacyl) site**; peptide transfer then occurs to it from the P site, and the uncharged tRNA leaves from the E site. Blocking the A site therefore stops elongation. Mnemonic for the order tRNAs pass through: **A → P → E**.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA', of:'In the absence of its inducer, an inducible operon is _____', kind:'prediction', q:'E. coli are grown on glycerol as the only carbon source, with no lactose present. What is the state of the lac operon?', choices:['Off, because the repressor is bound to the operator','On, because CAP–cAMP is bound near the promoter','Off, because the promoter is silenced by methylation','On, because allolactose has released the repressor'], ans:0, exp:'The lac operon is **inducible**: without lactose there is no allolactose, so the active repressor sits on the operator and blocks RNA polymerase. Low glucose does raise cAMP so CAP–cAMP may be bound, but activation cannot overcome a bound repressor.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA', of:'In the absence of its corepressor, a repressible operon is _____', kind:'scenario', q:'E. coli are moved to a medium that lacks tryptophan. What is the state of the trp operon?', choices:['On, because the repressor cannot bind the operator','Off, because the repressor is bound to the operator','On, because tryptophan activates RNA polymerase','Off, because no inducer is available to it'], ans:0, exp:'The trp operon is **repressible**: tryptophan is the corepressor that activates the repressor. With no tryptophan, the repressor is inactive and cannot bind the operator, so the cell transcribes the genes to make its own tryptophan. Inducers belong to inducible operons such as lac.'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA', of:'The regulatory protein that binds near an operon\'s promoter and increases RNA polymerase binding, as CAP–cAMP does for the lac operon, is the _____', kind:'prediction', q:'E. coli are grown with both glucose and lactose present. Why is lac operon transcription low?', choices:['Low cAMP leaves CAP unable to recruit RNA polymerase','The repressor stays bound to the operator','Glucose binds allolactose and inactivates it','High cAMP causes CAP to block the promoter'], ans:0, exp:'Lactose produces allolactose, which removes the repressor, but glucose keeps **cAMP low**, so the **activator CAP** cannot bind and recruit RNA polymerase efficiently (catabolite repression). Strong transcription requires both repressor removal (lactose present) and CAP–cAMP binding (glucose absent).'},
  {ofTag:'DNA & RNA', of:'Heavy methylation of the CpG islands in a gene\'s promoter _____ the access of transcription machinery to that DNA', kind:'prediction', q:'A tumor suppressor gene is silenced in a cancer without any change in its DNA sequence, and its promoter CpG islands are heavily methylated. Which drug would most likely restore its expression?', choices:['A DNA methyltransferase inhibitor','A histone acetyltransferase inhibitor','A topoisomerase II inhibitor','A reverse transcriptase inhibitor'], ans:0, exp:'**Promoter CpG methylation** compacts chromatin and blocks transcription factor binding, silencing the gene epigenetically. Inhibiting DNA methyltransferases (e.g., azacitidine) lets methylation be lost with replication and can reactivate the gene. Blocking histone acetylation would silence chromatin further.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'At low temperatures, cholesterol will _____ membrane fluidity', kind:'prediction', q:'Cholesterol is removed from the plasma membranes of cells kept at 4 °C. What happens to membrane fluidity?', choices:['It decreases, because phospholipids can now pack tightly','It increases, because cholesterol was stiffening the bilayer','It is unchanged, because cholesterol acts only when warm','It increases, because the fatty acid tails become shorter'], ans:0, exp:'At low temperature, **cholesterol** wedges between phospholipids and keeps their tails from packing into a gel, so it **raises fluidity**; removing it lets the membrane stiffen. At high temperature it does the opposite, restraining tail motion. Cholesterol is a bidirectional fluidity buffer.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'_____ seal adjacent epithelial cells together, preventing solutes from leaking between cells by the paracellular route, but do not allow exchange between the cells', kind:'scenario', q:'A bacterial toxin degrades claudins in the intestinal epithelium. What is the most likely result?', choices:['Solutes leak between cells by the paracellular route','Ions can no longer pass directly between cytoplasms','Cells detach from the underlying basement membrane','Epithelial sheets tear under mechanical stress'], ans:0, exp:'Claudins and occludins form **tight junctions**, the seals that block paracellular leakage and separate apical from basolateral membrane domains. Losing them makes the epithelium leaky. Direct cytoplasmic exchange is a gap junction function, and resistance to tearing comes from desmosomes.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'_____ allow rapid exchange of ions and other small molecules directly between the cytoplasm of adjacent cells', kind:'prediction', q:'A mutation reduces the number of connexin channels between cardiac muscle cells. What is the most likely consequence?', choices:['Slower spread of depolarization from cell to cell','Leakage of solutes between cells into the lumen','Weaker anchoring of intermediate filaments','Loss of adhesion to the basement membrane'], ans:0, exp:'Connexins form **gap junctions**, which let ions flow directly between neighboring cytoplasms; in the heart (at intercalated discs) this lets depolarization spread so the myocardium contracts as a functional syncytium. Fewer channels slow conduction and predispose to arrhythmias.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'_____ is the pressure that must be applied to a solution to stop the net flow of water into it across a semipermeable membrane', kind:'application', q:'Each solution is separated from pure water by a membrane permeable only to water at 25 °C. Which solution has the greatest osmotic pressure?', choices:['0.10 M CaCl₂','0.10 M NaCl','0.10 M glucose','0.15 M glucose'], ans:0, exp:'**Osmotic pressure** Π = iMRT depends on the total concentration of dissolved particles. CaCl₂ dissociates into 3 ions (0.30 M particles), NaCl into 2 (0.20 M), while glucose does not dissociate (0.10 or 0.15 M). Higher Π means water flows in more strongly.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'_____ is a form of passive transport that uses carrier or channel proteins to move solutes that cannot cross the bilayer on their own', kind:'prediction', q:'Glucose uptake through GLUT1 is measured at increasing extracellular glucose concentrations. Which observation distinguishes this from simple diffusion?', choices:['The uptake rate levels off at high glucose','The uptake rate rises linearly with glucose','Uptake continues against a concentration gradient','Uptake stops completely when ATP is depleted'], ans:0, exp:'**Facilitated diffusion** uses a limited number of carriers, so it shows **saturation kinetics** (a plateau, like Vmax), whereas simple diffusion rises linearly with concentration. Both are passive: neither can move solute against its gradient or needs ATP.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'In _____, the energy for transport comes directly from ATP hydrolysis', kind:'scenario', q:'Digoxin inhibits the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase in cardiac muscle cells. Which form of transport does it block directly?', choices:['Primary active transport','Secondary active transport','Facilitated diffusion','Simple diffusion'], ans:0, exp:'The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase hydrolyzes ATP itself to move 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in, so it is **primary active transport**. Digoxin\'s effect on contractility is indirect: the reduced Na⁺ gradient slows the Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger (secondary active), so more Ca²⁺ stays in the cell.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'_____ harnesses the energy released by one particle moving down its electrochemical gradient to drive a different particle up its gradient', kind:'prediction', q:'Ouabain is applied to intestinal epithelial cells to block the basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase. What happens to glucose uptake through the apical SGLT1 transporter?', choices:['It declines as the Na⁺ gradient runs down','It increases as more Na⁺ is kept outside','It is unchanged because SGLT1 uses no ATP','It switches to an ATP-driven pump mechanism'], ans:0, exp:'SGLT1 is **secondary active transport**: it uses Na⁺ moving down its gradient to carry glucose up its gradient. The gradient is maintained by the ATP-driven Na⁺/K⁺ pump, so blocking the pump lets the gradient run down and glucose uptake falls, even though SGLT1 itself never hydrolyzes ATP.'},
  {ofTag:'Cell Membrane', of:'In membrane physiology, the Nernst equation calculates the _____ of a single ion from its concentrations on the two sides of the membrane', kind:'application', q:'At 37 °C, [K⁺] is 5 mM outside a cell and 140 mM inside. Using the Nernst equation, what is the approximate equilibrium potential for K⁺?', choices:['−89 mV','+89 mV','−61 mV','−70 mV'], ans:0, exp:'**E = (61.5 mV/z) log([out]/[in])** = 61.5 × log(5/140) = 61.5 × (−1.45) ≈ −89 mV. It is negative because K⁺ is concentrated inside and tends to leave. The resting potential (about −70 mV) is near, but not equal to, E_K because some Na⁺ also leaks in.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Glycolysis', of:'_____ is the enzyme that catalyzes the committed, rate-limiting step of glycolysis', kind:'application', q:'A drug is designed to block glycolysis specifically, without blocking glycogen synthesis or the pentose phosphate pathway. Which reaction should it inhibit?', choices:['Fructose 6-phosphate → fructose 1,6-bisphosphate','Glucose → glucose 6-phosphate','Phosphoenolpyruvate → pyruvate','Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate → 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate'], ans:0, exp:'Glucose 6-phosphate is a branch point feeding glycogen synthesis and the pentose phosphate pathway, so the first step **committed only to glycolysis** is **phosphofructokinase-1** (F6P → F1,6BP), which is also its rate-limiting, most tightly regulated step. Blocking hexokinase would also starve the other pathways.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Glycolysis', of:'Phosphofructokinase-1 is allosterically activated by _____', kind:'prediction', q:'During intense muscle contraction, ATP is used faster than it is regenerated and AMP levels rise. How does this affect glycolytic flux?', choices:['It increases, because AMP activates phosphofructokinase-1','It decreases, because AMP inhibits phosphofructokinase-1','It increases, because AMP activates glucose 6-phosphatase','It decreases, because AMP inhibits pyruvate kinase'], ans:0, exp:'**AMP** signals low energy charge and allosterically **activates PFK-1**, relieving the inhibition by ATP so glycolysis speeds up to make more ATP. High ATP and citrate do the opposite. Glucose 6-phosphatase is a gluconeogenic enzyme absent from muscle.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Glycolysis', of:'Pyruvate kinase is allosterically inhibited by _____', kind:'except', q:'Hepatic pyruvate kinase is inhibited by all of the following EXCEPT:', choices:['Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate','High levels of ATP','Alanine','Phosphorylation by protein kinase A'], ans:0, exp:'Pyruvate kinase is turned down by signs of plentiful energy or fuel: **ATP**, **alanine**, and (in liver) glucagon-driven **PKA phosphorylation**, which spares PEP for gluconeogenesis. **Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate** is a feed-forward **activator**, linking pyruvate kinase to the PFK-1 step upstream.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Glycolysis', of:'Which glycolytic enzyme oxidizes glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate and adds inorganic phosphate to form 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate?', kind:'prediction', q:'In anaerobic skeletal muscle, lactate dehydrogenase is blocked. Which glycolytic enzyme stops first because its oxidized cofactor runs out?', choices:['Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase','Phosphoglycerate kinase','Triose phosphate isomerase','Phosphofructokinase-1'], ans:0, exp:'**GAPDH** is the only glycolytic step that uses NAD⁺, reducing it to NADH as it oxidizes G3P to 1,3-BPG. Without O₂, the only way to regenerate NAD⁺ is lactate dehydrogenase (pyruvate → lactate); block it and NAD⁺ is used up, halting GAPDH and therefore all ATP-producing steps downstream.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Glycolysis', of:'Which glycolytic enzyme converts 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate?', kind:'application', q:'In red blood cells, bisphosphoglycerate mutase converts some 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 2,3-BPG, which is later converted to 3-phosphoglycerate. What does this shunt cost the cell?', choices:['The ATP normally made by phosphoglycerate kinase','The NADH normally made by GAPDH','The ATP normally made by pyruvate kinase','An extra ATP spent at the hexokinase step'], ans:0, exp:'**Phosphoglycerate kinase** makes ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation as it converts 1,3-BPG to 3-phosphoglycerate. The Rapoport–Luebering shunt bypasses that step, so the ATP is lost, but the cell gains 2,3-BPG, which lowers hemoglobin\'s O₂ affinity. NADH from GAPDH is made before the branch point and is unaffected.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Glycolysis', of:'Which enzyme catalyzes the final, irreversible step of glycolysis, converting phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate?', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient with inherited pyruvate kinase deficiency has chronic hemolytic anemia while other tissues are largely spared. Why are red blood cells especially affected?', choices:['They rely entirely on glycolysis for ATP','They need pyruvate to synthesize heme','They use pyruvate kinase to make NADPH','They depend on pyruvate for oxidative phosphorylation'], ans:0, exp:'Mature RBCs have no mitochondria, so **glycolysis is their only ATP source**, and **pyruvate kinase** makes the final ATP of the pathway. Without enough ATP, RBCs cannot maintain their ion pumps and membrane shape, so they are destroyed in the spleen. NADPH comes from the pentose phosphate pathway, not pyruvate kinase.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis', of:'Because the pyruvate kinase step is irreversible, in gluconeogenesis pyruvate must first be converted to _____ before becoming phosphoenolpyruvate', kind:'scenario', q:'A child with pyruvate carboxylase deficiency develops fasting hypoglycemia and lactic acidosis. Which conversion is blocked, preventing gluconeogenesis from lactate and alanine?', choices:['Pyruvate → oxaloacetate','Oxaloacetate → phosphoenolpyruvate','Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate → fructose 6-phosphate','Glucose 6-phosphate → glucose'], ans:0, exp:'Because the pyruvate kinase step cannot run backward, gluconeogenesis bypasses it in two steps: **pyruvate carboxylase** (pyruvate → **oxaloacetate**, in mitochondria) and then PEPCK (OAA → PEP). Lactate and alanine both enter as pyruvate, so they cannot become glucose, and pyruvate backs up into lactate.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis', of:'Pyruvate carboxylase, the first enzyme of gluconeogenesis from pyruvate, is allosterically activated by _____', kind:'prediction', q:'During fasting, high rates of beta oxidation raise mitochondrial acetyl-CoA in the liver. How does this affect the fate of pyruvate?', choices:['It is directed into oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis','It is directed into acetyl-CoA for the citric acid cycle','It is directed into lactate for export to muscle','It is directed into alanine for protein synthesis'], ans:0, exp:'**Acetyl-CoA activates pyruvate carboxylase** and also inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase (by stimulating PDH kinase). So when fatty acids supply plenty of acetyl-CoA, pyruvate is sent to **oxaloacetate** for glucose synthesis rather than being burned. This is how fat oxidation supports, but cannot itself fuel, gluconeogenesis.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis', of:'In gluconeogenesis, which enzyme hydrolyzes fructose 1,6-bisphosphate to fructose 6-phosphate?', kind:'scenario', q:'A child with fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase deficiency develops hypoglycemia and lactic acidosis whenever she fasts. Which reaction can her liver not perform?', choices:['Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate → fructose 6-phosphate','Glucose 6-phosphate → glucose','Pyruvate → oxaloacetate','Fructose 6-phosphate → fructose 1,6-bisphosphate'], ans:0, exp:'**Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase** bypasses the irreversible PFK-1 step by hydrolyzing the C-1 phosphate; it is the rate-limiting enzyme of gluconeogenesis. Glycogen still supplies glucose early in a fast, so symptoms appear once glycogen runs low. The reverse reaction listed is PFK-1, a glycolytic enzyme.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Gluconeogenesis', of:'Which enzyme catalyzes the final step of gluconeogenesis, hydrolyzing glucose 6-phosphate to free glucose?', kind:'application', q:'Why can the liver, but not skeletal muscle, release glucose from its glycogen stores into the blood?', choices:['Only liver expresses glucose 6-phosphatase','Only liver expresses glycogen phosphorylase','Only muscle lacks glucose transporters','Only liver expresses debranching enzyme'], ans:0, exp:'Glycogen breakdown yields glucose 1-phosphate, then glucose 6-phosphate, which is trapped in the cell by its charge. Only liver (and kidney) has **glucose 6-phosphatase** in the ER to remove the phosphate so glucose can exit. Muscle has phosphorylase and debranching enzyme but keeps its G6P for its own glycolysis.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA', of:'The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex is turned off when it is _____', kind:'prediction', q:'Dichloroacetate inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase. What happens to the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH)?', choices:['It stays dephosphorylated and more active','It stays phosphorylated and less active','It is degraded more quickly by proteasomes','It is unaffected because the kinase activates it'], ans:0, exp:'**PDH kinase phosphorylates and inactivates** PDH; PDH phosphatase reactivates it. Blocking the kinase leaves PDH dephosphorylated and active, so more pyruvate becomes acetyl-CoA instead of lactate. That is why dichloroacetate has been tried in lactic acidosis.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle', of:'The rate-limiting enzyme of the citric acid cycle is _____', kind:'application', q:'When a cell has high ATP and NADH, citrate accumulates in mitochondria and is exported to the cytosol for fatty acid synthesis. Inhibition of which enzyme causes this buildup?', choices:['Isocitrate dehydrogenase','Citrate synthase','Malate dehydrogenase','Succinate dehydrogenase'], ans:0, exp:'**Isocitrate dehydrogenase**, the rate-limiting step of the cycle, is inhibited by ATP and NADH and activated by ADP and Ca²⁺. When it slows, isocitrate and citrate (which are in equilibrium through aconitase) build up, and citrate leaves to supply acetyl-CoA for lipogenesis. Inhibiting citrate synthase would lower citrate instead.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: TCA Cycle', of:'Which citric acid cycle enzyme converts α-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA?', kind:'scenario', q:'In thiamine deficiency (as in Wernicke encephalopathy), α-ketoglutarate builds up in the brain. Which enzyme cannot work without the missing cofactor?', choices:['α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex','Isocitrate dehydrogenase','Succinyl-CoA synthetase','Succinate dehydrogenase'], ans:0, exp:'The **α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex**, like pyruvate dehydrogenase, needs **thiamine pyrophosphate (B1)**, lipoate, CoA, FAD and NAD⁺ to decarboxylate α-KG to succinyl-CoA. Thiamine deficiency therefore blocks it and starves glucose-dependent neurons of energy. Isocitrate dehydrogenase uses only NAD⁺.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: ETC', of:'Complex II in the ETC is also known as _____', kind:'prediction', q:'Malonate, a competitive inhibitor of succinate dehydrogenase, is added to isolated mitochondria. Which ETC complex loses its electron input?', choices:['Complex II','Complex I','Complex III','Complex IV'], ans:0, exp:'**Succinate dehydrogenase is Complex II**: it oxidizes succinate to fumarate in the citric acid cycle and passes the electrons through FADH₂ to ubiquinone. Malonate resembles succinate and blocks that active site. Electrons from NADH still enter at Complex I.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: ETC', of:'Complex II pumps _____ protons into the intermembrane space for each pair of electrons it transfers', kind:'application', q:'Electrons from succinate enter the ETC at Complex II, while electrons from NADH enter at Complex I. Why do succinate-derived electrons support less ATP synthesis?', choices:['They bypass Complex I, a proton-pumping step','They bypass Complex IV, the final proton pump','They pass to O₂ without going through ubiquinone','They reduce cytochrome c without passing Complex III'], ans:0, exp:'**Complex II pumps no protons**. Electrons from FADH₂ join at ubiquinone, skipping Complex I (4 H⁺ per pair), so they drive only Complexes III and IV, about 6 H⁺ versus 10 H⁺ per pair. That is why FADH₂ yields about 1.5 ATP versus about 2.5 for NADH.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: ETC', of:'_____ is the final acceptor of electrons in the ETC during aerobic respiration', kind:'scenario', q:'In cyanide poisoning, Complex IV cannot pass electrons to their final acceptor. What happens in the mitochondria?', choices:['Electron flow and ATP synthesis stop as the chain stays reduced','Electron flow speeds up, but ATP synthesis stops','The proton gradient grows because pumping continues','NADH is rapidly oxidized back to NAD⁺ in the matrix'], ans:0, exp:'**O₂ is the final electron acceptor**, reduced to water at Complex IV. Cyanide blocks that step, so every carrier upstream stays reduced, electron flow and proton pumping stop, and ATP synthesis collapses. NADH accumulates, cells shift to anaerobic glycolysis, and lactic acidosis follows.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: ETC', of:'_____ uses a proton gradient to power the conversion of ADP to ATP', kind:'prediction', q:'Oligomycin blocks the proton channel of ATP synthase in tightly coupled mitochondria. What happens to O₂ consumption?', choices:['It falls, because the proton gradient builds and slows the ETC','It rises, because electrons now flow faster to O₂','It is unchanged, because the ETC works independently','It rises, because protons leak back into the matrix'], ans:0, exp:'**ATP synthase** lets protons flow back into the matrix and uses that energy to make ATP. When it is blocked, the proton gradient grows until pumping against it becomes too hard, so electron transport and O₂ use slow down. This respiratory control is the hallmark of coupled mitochondria; uncouplers do the opposite.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: ETC', of:'_____ is the movement of protons down their electrochemical gradient across a membrane, coupled to ATP synthesis by ATP synthase', kind:'prediction', q:'2,4-Dinitrophenol carries protons across the inner mitochondrial membrane, bypassing ATP synthase. What is the effect?', choices:['ATP synthesis falls; O₂ use and heat rise','ATP synthesis rises; O₂ use and heat fall','ATP synthesis and O₂ use both fall','ATP synthesis and O₂ use both rise'], ans:0, exp:'In **chemiosmosis**, ATP synthase uses the proton gradient made by the ETC. An **uncoupler** dissipates the gradient, so less ATP is made, but the ETC runs faster (no back-pressure), consuming more O₂ and releasing the energy as **heat**. Thermogenin (UCP1) in brown fat uses the same principle.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'The rate-limiting, regulated enzyme of fatty acid synthesis is _____', kind:'prediction', q:'After a carbohydrate-rich meal, insulin promotes dephosphorylation of acetyl-CoA carboxylase, and citrate builds up in the cytosol. What is the result?', choices:['Malonyl-CoA rises and fatty acid synthesis increases','Malonyl-CoA falls and beta oxidation increases','Malonyl-CoA rises and beta oxidation increases','Malonyl-CoA falls and ketogenesis increases'], ans:0, exp:'**Acetyl-CoA carboxylase**, the rate-limiting step of fatty acid synthesis, is activated by citrate and by insulin-driven dephosphorylation, and inhibited by glucagon/AMPK phosphorylation and by palmitoyl-CoA. More ACC activity means more **malonyl-CoA**, which feeds fatty acid synthase and also blocks beta oxidation.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA', of:'Acyl-CoA must use the _____ to pass through the inner mitochondrial membrane', kind:'prediction', q:'Malonyl-CoA levels rise in liver cells after a meal. What happens to beta oxidation of long-chain fatty acids, and why?', choices:['It falls, because malonyl-CoA inhibits carnitine acyltransferase I','It rises, because malonyl-CoA activates carnitine acyltransferase I','It falls, because malonyl-CoA inhibits acyl-CoA dehydrogenase','It is unchanged, because the two pathways are in separate compartments'], ans:0, exp:'Long-chain acyl-CoA enters the matrix only by the **carnitine shuttle**, whose first enzyme, carnitine acyltransferase I (CPT-I), is inhibited by malonyl-CoA. This keeps newly made fatty acids from being burned as soon as they are made. Separate compartments matter precisely because the shuttle is the regulated gate between them.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Acetyl-CoA', of:'Acetyl-CoA uses the _____ to exit the mitochondria', kind:'scenario', q:'A drug blocks the mitochondrial citrate transporter in hepatocytes of an animal fed a high-carbohydrate diet. Which process is most directly reduced?', choices:['Fatty acid synthesis in the cytosol','Beta oxidation in the matrix','Ketone body synthesis in the matrix','Glycogen synthesis in the cytosol'], ans:0, exp:'Acetyl-CoA cannot cross the inner membrane, so it leaves as **citrate** (the citrate shuttle), which ATP-citrate lyase splits back into acetyl-CoA and oxaloacetate in the cytosol. Fatty acid synthesis is cytosolic, so blocking citrate export starves it of carbon. Beta oxidation and ketogenesis occur inside the matrix.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'A C16 fatty acid will undergo a maximum of _____ rounds of beta oxidation', kind:'application', q:'Palmitoyl-CoA (16 carbons, saturated) is completely broken down by beta oxidation. How many FADH₂ and NADH are produced?', choices:['7 FADH₂ and 7 NADH','8 FADH₂ and 8 NADH','7 FADH₂ and 8 NADH','8 FADH₂ and 7 NADH'], ans:0, exp:'A C16 chain needs **7 rounds** (n/2 − 1) to give 8 acetyl-CoA, because the last round splits a 4-carbon unit into two acetyl-CoA. Each round yields one FADH₂ (acyl-CoA dehydrogenase) and one NADH (3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase), so 7 of each.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: PPP', of:'The rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway is _____', kind:'scenario', q:'A man develops hemolytic anemia with Heinz bodies after taking primaquine. Deficiency of which enzyme, and loss of which product, best explains this?', choices:['Glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase; NADPH','Pyruvate kinase; ATP','Hexokinase; glucose 6-phosphate','Transketolase; ribose 5-phosphate'], ans:0, exp:'**G6PD**, the rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway, is the RBC\'s only source of **NADPH**, which keeps glutathione reduced to neutralize oxidants. Oxidant drugs then damage hemoglobin (Heinz bodies) and membranes. Pyruvate kinase deficiency also causes hemolysis, but it is not triggered by oxidant drugs.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'During prolonged starvation, ketone bodies form in the liver because of excess _____', kind:'prediction', q:'During a prolonged fast, hepatic oxaloacetate is drawn off for gluconeogenesis while beta oxidation remains rapid. What happens to the acetyl-CoA that cannot enter the citric acid cycle?', choices:['It is converted into ketone bodies','It is converted into glucose','It is converted into malonyl-CoA','It is converted into pyruvate'], ans:0, exp:'Acetyl-CoA needs oxaloacetate to enter the cycle as citrate. When OAA is diverted to gluconeogenesis, the **excess acetyl-CoA** is converted in liver mitochondria into **ketone bodies** for export. Acetyl-CoA cannot become pyruvate or glucose because the PDH reaction is irreversible.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'During prolonged starvation, the brain can derive up to about two-thirds of its energy from _____', kind:'except', q:'Which statement about brain fuel use after several weeks of starvation is NOT correct?', choices:['The brain oxidizes fatty acids as its main fuel','The brain takes up acetoacetate and 3-hydroxybutyrate','The brain still oxidizes some glucose','Ketone use by the brain spares muscle protein'], ans:0, exp:'Long-chain fatty acids cross the blood–brain barrier poorly and are not a major brain fuel. In prolonged starvation the brain switches to **ketone bodies** for up to about two-thirds of its energy while still using some glucose. This lowers the need for gluconeogenesis from amino acids, sparing muscle protein.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'_____ occurs when insulin deficiency causes very high blood glucose together with a dangerous buildup of acidic ketone bodies', kind:'scenario', q:'A 19-year-old with type 1 diabetes misses several insulin doses and presents with glucose of 550 mg/dL, deep rapid breathing and a fruity breath odor. What best explains his acidosis?', choices:['Unrestrained lipolysis drives hepatic ketone body production','Anaerobic glycolysis drives lactate production in muscle','Hypoventilation causes CO₂ to be retained in the blood','Severe diarrhea causes loss of bicarbonate from the gut'], ans:0, exp:'Without insulin, lipolysis is unchecked and glucagon dominates, so the liver turns the flood of fatty acids into acetoacetate and 3-hydroxybutyrate: **diabetic ketoacidosis**. The deep (Kussmaul) breathing is respiratory compensation, and the fruity odor is acetone. Hypoventilation would worsen, not explain, the pattern.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'_____ is the rate-limiting enzyme of the mevalonate pathway, which produces cholesterol', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient starts atorvastatin. By what mechanism does the drug lower plasma LDL?', choices:['It blocks liver cholesterol synthesis, so hepatocytes make more LDL receptors','It blocks gut cholesterol absorption, so chylomicrons carry less cholesterol','It activates lipoprotein lipase, so VLDL triglycerides are cleared faster','It binds bile acids in the gut, so the liver converts more cholesterol'], ans:0, exp:'Statins inhibit **HMG-CoA reductase**, the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis. Lower intracellular cholesterol activates SREBP, which increases **LDL receptor** expression, so the liver pulls more LDL from the blood. Ezetimibe blocks absorption, and bile acid resins bind bile acids.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'The main function of low-density lipoproteins (LDL) is to _____', kind:'prediction', q:'A patient with familial hypercholesterolemia has defective LDL receptors. What is the main consequence?', choices:['Cholesterol-rich LDL accumulates in the plasma','Triglyceride-rich chylomicrons accumulate after meals','HDL can no longer pick up peripheral cholesterol','VLDL can no longer be secreted from the liver'], ans:0, exp:'**LDL** delivers cholesterol to tissues by binding the LDL receptor (via ApoB-100) and being taken up by endocytosis. Without working receptors, LDL cannot leave the blood, so plasma cholesterol rises and causes early atherosclerosis and xanthomas. Chylomicrons are cleared by lipoprotein lipase and ApoE-dependent pathways instead.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'In intestinal cells, absorbed long-chain fatty acids are re-esterified to triacylglycerols and packaged into _____ for release into the lymphatic system', kind:'scenario', q:'In abetalipoproteinemia, enterocytes cannot assemble ApoB-containing lipoproteins, and fat accumulates inside them. Which particle can these cells not produce?', choices:['Chylomicrons','LDL','HDL','IDL'], ans:0, exp:'Enterocytes re-esterify absorbed fatty acids into triacylglycerols and package them with **ApoB-48** into **chylomicrons**, which enter the lacteals. Without ApoB assembly, dietary fat is trapped in the enterocytes, causing steatorrhea and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency. LDL and IDL arise in plasma from VLDL, not from the intestine.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Glycogen', of:'In glycogenolysis, glycogen phosphorylase releases glucose 1-phosphate by breaking _____', kind:'prediction', q:'Glycogen phosphorylase acts on a highly branched glycogen particle. Where does it stop, and what is needed for breakdown to continue?', choices:['Four residues from a branch point; debranching enzyme','At the branch point itself; glycogen synthase','At the reducing end; glucose 6-phosphatase','Two residues from a branch point; branching enzyme'], ans:0, exp:'**Glycogen phosphorylase** cleaves **α-1,4 bonds** by phosphorolysis, releasing glucose 1-phosphate from the chain ends, but it cannot get closer than about four residues to an α-1,6 branch. **Debranching enzyme** then moves three residues to another chain and hydrolyzes the α-1,6 bond, releasing one free glucose.'},
  {ofTag:'Metabolism: Urea Cycle', of:'When amino acids are degraded, their amino groups are released as _____, which the liver converts into urea', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient with cirrhosis becomes confused and develops a flapping tremor after a large protein meal. Accumulation of which compound, normally converted to urea by the liver, is responsible?', choices:['Ammonia','Uric acid','Creatinine','Lactate'], ans:0, exp:'Amino groups from protein breakdown are released as **ammonia (NH₃/NH₄⁺)**, which the liver detoxifies through the urea cycle. In liver failure ammonia accumulates and crosses into the brain, causing hepatic encephalopathy (confusion, asterixis). Uric acid comes from purine breakdown and creatinine from creatine.'},
  {ofTag:'Lipid Metabolism', of:'Emulsification of dietary lipids in the small intestine is aided mainly by _____', kind:'prediction', q:'A gallstone blocks the common bile duct. Absorption of which nutrients is most impaired?', choices:['Dietary fats and fat-soluble vitamins','Monosaccharides such as glucose','Amino acids and small peptides','Water-soluble vitamins such as vitamin C'], ans:0, exp:'**Bile salts** are amphipathic: they emulsify fat droplets so pancreatic lipase can act and then form micelles that carry fatty acids and vitamins A, D, E and K to the enterocytes. Without bile, fat passes into the stool (steatorrhea) and fat-soluble vitamins become deficient, while sugar and protein absorption are largely spared.'},
  {ofTag:'Reproduction: Cell Cycle', of:'At the _____ checkpoint (restriction point), cells decide whether to commit to division', kind:'prediction', q:'DNA damage is detected in a cell before it begins replicating its DNA; p53 rises and induces p21, which inhibits cyclin–CDK complexes. Where does the cell arrest?', choices:['At the G1/S transition, before DNA replication','At the G2/M transition, after DNA replication','At the metaphase–anaphase transition','In late telophase, before cytokinesis'], ans:0, exp:'The **G1 checkpoint (restriction point)** decides whether a cell commits to S phase; p53–p21 blocks the CDKs that would phosphorylate Rb and release E2F, so the cell pauses in G1 for repair or undergoes apoptosis. Damage found after replication is handled at the G2 checkpoint instead.'},
  {ofTag:'Reproduction: Cell Cycle', of:'At the _____ checkpoint, the cell verifies that its DNA has been replicated completely and correctly before mitosis', kind:'scenario', q:'A cell finishes S phase, but several replication forks stalled and left stretches of DNA uncopied. Which checkpoint should keep this cell from entering mitosis?', choices:['G2 checkpoint','G1 checkpoint','Spindle (M) checkpoint','Cytokinesis checkpoint'], ans:0, exp:'The **G2 checkpoint** checks that replication is complete and the DNA undamaged before the cell commits to mitosis (by holding back cyclin B–CDK1). The G1 checkpoint acts before replication, and the spindle checkpoint checks kinetochore attachment in metaphase.'},
  {ofTag:'Reproduction: Meiosis', of:'Failure of homologous chromosomes or sister chromatids to separate properly during anaphase is called _____', kind:'scenario', q:'A 42-year-old woman has a child with trisomy 21, and genetic testing shows the egg carried both of her copies of chromosome 21. What error occurred?', choices:['Nondisjunction during meiosis','Crossing over during prophase I','A reciprocal translocation','A deletion on chromosome 21'], ans:0, exp:'**Nondisjunction** (homologs failing to separate in anaphase I, or sister chromatids in anaphase II) yields a gamete with an extra chromosome; fertilization then gives trisomy. Its risk rises sharply with maternal age because oocytes stay arrested in prophase I for decades. Crossing over exchanges segments but does not change chromosome number.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'A male who inherits one mutant allele for an X-linked recessive disorder _____', kind:'prediction', q:'A woman who carries hemophilia A (X-linked recessive) has children with an unaffected man. What fraction of their sons are expected to be affected?', choices:['1/2','1/4','1','0'], ans:0, exp:'Sons get their only X from their mother, and a carrier mother passes the mutant X half the time. Because males are **hemizygous**, one mutant allele is enough to express the disease, so half the sons are affected. The 1/4 figure is the chance for all children combined, not for sons.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'A female who inherits one mutant allele for an X-linked recessive disorder is usually _____', kind:'scenario', q:'A man with red–green color blindness (X-linked recessive) has children with a woman who carries no mutant allele. What is expected for their daughters?', choices:['All are carriers who usually see normally','All are color-blind','Half are carriers and half are noncarriers','None inherit the mutant allele'], ans:0, exp:'A father passes his single X to every daughter, so all daughters receive the mutant allele, but their normal maternal X masks it, making them **unaffected carriers**. His sons receive his Y and are unaffected. Male-to-male transmission never occurs for X-linked traits.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'_____ is the proportion of individuals carrying a particular gene variant who also express the associated trait', kind:'application', q:'Among women who carry a pathogenic BRCA1 variant, roughly 70% develop breast cancer by age 80. This percentage describes the variant\'s:', choices:['Penetrance','Expressivity','Pleiotropy','Epistasis'], ans:0, exp:'**Penetrance** is the fraction of carriers who show the phenotype at all; values below 100% are called incomplete penetrance. **Expressivity** instead describes how severely the trait appears among those who do show it.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'_____ is when a phenotype is expressed to different degrees among individuals with the same genotype', kind:'scenario', q:'Relatives who share the same neurofibromatosis type 1 mutation differ widely: one has only a few café-au-lait spots, while another has hundreds of neurofibromas. What does this illustrate?', choices:['Variable expressivity','Incomplete penetrance','Codominance','Epistasis'], ans:0, exp:'Everyone here shows the trait, but to **different degrees**, which is **variable expressivity**. Incomplete penetrance would mean some carriers show no phenotype at all. Codominance requires two different alleles to be expressed side by side.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'_____ is when both alleles are fully and simultaneously expressed in the heterozygote', kind:'application', q:'A person with blood type AB has both A and B antigens fully expressed on their red blood cells. Which inheritance pattern does this show?', choices:['Codominance','Incomplete dominance','Complete dominance','Epistasis'], ans:0, exp:'In **codominance** each allele makes its own product and both appear fully in the heterozygote, as with Iᴬ and Iᴮ. Incomplete dominance would instead give a blended, intermediate phenotype. The ABO gene also shows complete dominance of Iᴬ and Iᴮ over i.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'_____ is when a heterozygote shows an intermediate (blended) phenotype, as in pink snapdragons from red and white parents', kind:'prediction', q:'Two pink-flowered snapdragons are crossed; flower color shows incomplete dominance of red over white. What phenotypic ratio is expected in the offspring?', choices:['1 red : 2 pink : 1 white','3 red : 1 white','3 pink : 1 white','1 pink : 1 white'], ans:0, exp:'Pink plants are heterozygous (Rr). An Rr × Rr cross gives 1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr, and with **incomplete dominance** each genotype has its own phenotype, so the phenotypic ratio equals the genotypic ratio: 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white. A 3:1 ratio would require complete dominance.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'The _____ two genes are on the same chromosome, the more likely they are to be separated by crossing over and to appear to assort independently', kind:'prediction', q:'On one chromosome, genes A and B are 5 map units apart, and genes A and C are 40 map units apart. Which statement is correct?', choices:['A and C recombine more often than A and B','A and B recombine more often than A and C','A and C are more tightly linked than A and B','A and B assort fully independently'], ans:0, exp:'Map distance reflects **recombination frequency**: the farther apart two genes are, the more likely a crossover falls between them. So A and C (40 cM) recombine about 40% of the time, versus about 5% for A and B, which are tightly linked. Recombination frequency never exceeds 50%, the value for independent assortment.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'For a gene with two alleles at frequencies p and q, which expression gives the Hardy–Weinberg genotype frequencies?', kind:'application', q:'In a population in Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium, 1 in 400 people has an autosomal recessive disease. About what percentage of the population are carriers?', choices:['9.5%','5%','0.25%','19%'], ans:0, exp:'Affected people are **q²** = 1/400, so q = 0.05 and p = 0.95. Carriers are **2pq** = 2 × 0.95 × 0.05 = 0.095, or about 9.5%. The 5% is just q, and 0.25% is the affected frequency itself.'},
  {ofTag:'Genetics', of:'The _____ is the loss of genetic variation that occurs when a new population is established by a very small number of individuals', kind:'scenario', q:'An isolated religious community, founded by about 200 settlers, has a far higher frequency of Ellis–van Creveld syndrome than the general population. What best explains this?', choices:['Founder effect','Bottleneck effect','Gene flow','Natural selection'], ans:0, exp:'When a few individuals start a new population, their alleles, including rare ones, are overrepresented by chance: the **founder effect**, a form of genetic drift. A bottleneck is similar but follows a drastic reduction of an existing population. Gene flow would dilute, not concentrate, the allele.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'When a single presynaptic neuron fires repeatedly in rapid succession so that its postsynaptic potentials add up and reach threshold, it is called _____', kind:'scenario', q:'One presynaptic neuron produces a subthreshold EPSP. When it fires three times within a few milliseconds, the postsynaptic neuron reaches threshold. What explains this?', choices:['Temporal summation','Spatial summation','Long-term potentiation','Saltatory conduction'], ans:0, exp:'**Temporal summation**: repeated inputs from the **same** synapse arrive before the earlier EPSPs decay, so they add up to threshold. Spatial summation is the adding of inputs from **different** synapses at the same moment.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'When several presynaptic neurons release neurotransmitter at the same time and their combined input triggers an action potential, it is called _____', kind:'prediction', q:'A neuron resting at −70 mV with a threshold of −55 mV receives simultaneous inputs: two excitatory synapses of +5 mV each and one inhibitory synapse of −8 mV. What happens?', choices:['No action potential, since the summed change is only +2 mV','An action potential fires, since excitatory inputs outnumber inhibitory ones','An action potential fires, since the membrane reaches −60 mV','No action potential, since inhibitory inputs always override'], ans:0, exp:'In **spatial summation** the axon hillock adds all simultaneous inputs: +5 + 5 − 8 = +2 mV, so the membrane reaches only −68 mV, well short of −55 mV. It is the net sum that matters, not the number of excitatory synapses.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'Once threshold is reached, making the stimulus stronger causes the action potential amplitude to _____', kind:'prediction', q:'A stimulus well above threshold is applied to an axon in place of one just at threshold. How do the resulting action potentials differ?', choices:['Same amplitude, but they may fire more often','Larger amplitude, but they fire at the same rate','Larger amplitude and faster conduction','Smaller amplitude and a longer refractory period'], ans:0, exp:'Action potentials are **all-or-none**: once threshold is crossed, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels drive a spike of fixed size. Stimulus strength is encoded by **firing frequency** (and by how many neurons are recruited), not by spike amplitude.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'During the rising phase of an action potential, Na⁺ enters the neuron through _____', kind:'scenario', q:'Tetrodotoxin from pufferfish blocks voltage-gated Na⁺ channels. What is its effect on a neuron?', choices:['Action potentials can no longer be generated','The resting potential becomes much more positive','Repolarization becomes markedly prolonged','Neurotransmitter release becomes continuous'], ans:0, exp:'The upstroke of the action potential depends on **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels** opening and letting Na⁺ rush in. Blocking them abolishes action potentials, causing paralysis. The resting potential is set mainly by K⁺ leak channels and is barely changed.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'Repolarization occurs when _____ ions exit the neuron', kind:'prediction', q:'A neuron is exposed to tetraethylammonium, which blocks voltage-gated K⁺ channels. How does its action potential change?', choices:['Repolarization slows and the spike is prolonged','The upstroke is abolished completely','The resting potential becomes more negative','The threshold becomes more negative'], ans:0, exp:'After the peak, **K⁺ efflux** through voltage-gated K⁺ channels returns the membrane toward rest. Blocking those channels leaves only slower leak currents, so the spike is broadened and prolonged. The upstroke, carried by Na⁺, still occurs.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'Hyperpolarization after an action potential occurs because voltage-gated K⁺ channels are _____', kind:'application', q:'Why does the membrane potential briefly dip below the resting potential right after an action potential?', choices:['Voltage-gated K⁺ channels close slowly, so K⁺ keeps leaving','Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels reopen and Na⁺ flows out','The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase briefly stops pumping ions','Cl⁻ rushes out of the cell through leak channels'], ans:0, exp:'Voltage-gated K⁺ channels are **slow to close**, so extra K⁺ continues to leave after repolarization and the membrane moves closer to E_K (about −90 mV) than at rest: **after-hyperpolarization**. This contributes to the relative refractory period.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'The resting potential is kept near the K⁺ equilibrium potential because the membrane has _____ K⁺ leak channels than Na⁺ leak channels', kind:'prediction', q:'Extracellular K⁺ rises from 4 mM to 8 mM (hyperkalemia). What happens to the resting membrane potential of nerve and muscle cells?', choices:['It depolarizes, becoming less negative','It hyperpolarizes, becoming more negative','It is unchanged, because Na⁺ sets the resting potential','It reverses and becomes positive'], ans:0, exp:'Because the resting membrane has **many more K⁺ leak channels** than Na⁺ leak channels, the resting potential sits near E_K. Raising outside K⁺ shrinks the K⁺ gradient, so E_K and the resting potential both become less negative, which is why hyperkalemia causes dangerous cardiac arrhythmias.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Synapses', of:'_____ enters the presynaptic terminal and causes vesicles containing neurotransmitter to fuse with the axon terminal membrane', kind:'scenario', q:'In Lambert–Eaton syndrome, antibodies block presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels at the neuromuscular junction. What is the direct result?', choices:['Less acetylcholine is released per action potential','Acetylcholine receptors on the muscle are destroyed','Acetylcholinesterase becomes more active','The motor axon can no longer conduct action potentials'], ans:0, exp:'When the action potential reaches the terminal, **Ca²⁺ influx** triggers synaptic vesicles to fuse with the membrane and release transmitter. With fewer Ca²⁺ channels, less acetylcholine is released, causing weakness. Destruction of ACh receptors is the mechanism of myasthenia gravis, not Lambert–Eaton.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Action Potential', of:'An inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) typically _____ the postsynaptic cell', kind:'prediction', q:'A neurotransmitter opens Cl⁻ channels on a mature neuron whose resting potential is −65 mV and whose Cl⁻ equilibrium potential is −75 mV. What is the effect?', choices:['It hyperpolarizes the cell, so firing is less likely','It depolarizes the cell, so firing is more likely','It has no effect, because Cl⁻ is already at equilibrium','It triggers an action potential directly'], ans:0, exp:'Opening Cl⁻ channels moves the membrane toward E_Cl (−75 mV), so Cl⁻ enters and the cell **hyperpolarizes**, moving it further from threshold: an **IPSP**. This is how GABA-A and glycine receptors inhibit neurons.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Neurotransmitters', of:'Ionotropic receptors respond to a neurotransmitter by _____', kind:'application', q:'The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor at the neuromuscular junction depolarizes the muscle within about a millisecond of acetylcholine binding. What explains this speed?', choices:['The receptor is itself a ligand-gated ion channel','The receptor activates a G protein that opens channels','The receptor phosphorylates nearby channels via a kinase','The receptor raises cAMP, which opens channels'], ans:0, exp:'Nicotinic receptors are **ionotropic**: the binding site and the cation channel are parts of one protein, so binding opens the pore at once. Metabotropic receptors act through G proteins and second messengers, which is slower but longer-lasting.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Neurotransmitters', of:'Metabotropic receptors respond to a neurotransmitter by _____', kind:'application', q:'Acetylcholine slows the heart by binding M2 muscarinic receptors, which signal through Gi and its βγ subunits to open K⁺ channels. What kind of receptor is M2?', choices:['A metabotropic receptor','An ionotropic receptor','A receptor tyrosine kinase','A nuclear hormone receptor'], ans:0, exp:'Muscarinic receptors are **metabotropic** G protein-coupled receptors: the receptor itself has no pore and acts through a G protein, so the response is slower but can be amplified and prolonged. The same transmitter acts ionotropically at nicotinic receptors.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Structure', of:'_____ produce myelin in the CNS', kind:'scenario', q:'In multiple sclerosis, demyelinating plaques form in the brain and spinal cord. Which cells make the myelin that is lost?', choices:['Oligodendrocytes','Schwann cells','Astrocytes','Microglia'], ans:0, exp:'**Oligodendrocytes** myelinate CNS axons, each wrapping segments of many axons, and they are the target in multiple sclerosis. Schwann cells myelinate peripheral nerves (the target in Guillain–Barré syndrome). Microglia are immune cells that may contribute to damage but do not make myelin.'},
  {ofTag:'Nervous System: Neurotransmitters', of:'The neurotransmitter released at the skeletal neuromuscular junction is _____', kind:'scenario', q:'In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies attack postsynaptic receptors at the skeletal neuromuscular junction, causing weakness that worsens with use. Which neurotransmitter is less effective?', choices:['Acetylcholine','Norepinephrine','Glutamate','GABA'], ans:0, exp:'Motor neurons release **acetylcholine**, which binds nicotinic receptors on the motor end plate. Antibodies against these receptors reduce the end-plate response, and acetylcholinesterase inhibitors help by letting ACh persist longer. Glutamate is the main excitatory CNS transmitter, not the NMJ transmitter.'},
  {ofTag:'Muscular System: Contraction', of:'During muscle contraction, Ca²⁺ binds to _____, causing tropomyosin to shift and exposing the myosin-binding sites on actin', kind:'prediction', q:'A mutation prevents troponin C from binding Ca²⁺. What happens when this muscle fiber is stimulated?', choices:['Tropomyosin keeps covering actin, so no cross-bridges form','Myosin heads stay locked onto actin in rigor','Ca²⁺ is not released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum','The sarcomere contracts and cannot relax'], ans:0, exp:'Ca²⁺ must bind **troponin** to shift tropomyosin off the myosin-binding sites on actin. Without that step tropomyosin stays in its blocking position, so cross-bridges cannot form even though Ca²⁺ is released normally. Rigor reflects a lack of ATP, not a lack of Ca²⁺ binding.'},
  {ofTag:'Muscular System: Contraction', of:'_____ binds to the myosin head, allowing it to release from actin', kind:'scenario', q:'After death, ATP is depleted and skeletal muscles become rigid (rigor mortis). What causes this rigidity?', choices:['Myosin heads cannot detach from actin without ATP','Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum','Tropomyosin covers every binding site on actin','Acetylcholine remains bound at the motor end plate'], ans:0, exp:'In the cross-bridge cycle, **ATP binding** releases the myosin head from actin; ATP hydrolysis then re-cocks it. Without ATP, myosin stays bound to actin and the muscle locks. Pumping Ca²⁺ back into the SR also needs ATP, so Ca²⁺ stays high and the binding sites stay exposed.'},
  {ofTag:'Muscular System: Contraction', of:'In muscle contraction, depolarization spreads along the sarcolemma into the T-tubules, triggering release of _____ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum', kind:'scenario', q:'In malignant hyperthermia, a mutant ryanodine receptor releases Ca²⁺ uncontrollably after exposure to certain anesthetics. What follows directly?', choices:['Sustained contraction from Ca²⁺ released by the SR','Flaccid paralysis from blocked acetylcholine release','Loss of depolarization along the T-tubules','Failure of troponin to bind to Ca²⁺'], ans:0, exp:'T-tubule depolarization normally opens ryanodine receptors so the **sarcoplasmic reticulum** releases **Ca²⁺**, which starts contraction. A leaky receptor floods the cytosol with Ca²⁺, causing rigidity and huge ATP use and heat production. Dantrolene treats it by blocking ryanodine receptor Ca²⁺ release.'},
  {ofTag:'Muscular System: Contraction', of:'In muscle, _____ donates a phosphate group to ADP, rapidly regenerating ATP', kind:'prediction', q:'During the first few seconds of a 100-meter sprint, which source regenerates ATP most rapidly in skeletal muscle?', choices:['Creatine phosphate','Fatty acid oxidation','Aerobic glucose oxidation','Ketone body oxidation'], ans:0, exp:'**Creatine phosphate** transfers its phosphate to ADP in a single step catalyzed by creatine kinase, the fastest way to replace ATP, but stores last only about 10 seconds. Anaerobic glycolysis takes over next, and oxidative pathways, though high in yield, are too slow for the first seconds.'},
  {ofTag:'Muscular System', of:'_____ skeletal muscle fibers are slow-twitch and rely on oxidative phosphorylation', kind:'application', q:'Elite marathon runners have a high proportion of leg muscle fibers rich in myoglobin and mitochondria. What type of fibers are these?', choices:['Red, slow-twitch oxidative fibers','White, fast-twitch glycolytic fibers','White, slow-twitch glycolytic fibers','Red, fast-twitch anaerobic fibers'], ans:0, exp:'**Red (type I) fibers** owe their color to myoglobin and rely on **oxidative phosphorylation**, so they contract slowly but resist fatigue, which suits endurance events. White (type IIb) fibers have little myoglobin, use anaerobic glycolysis, and are fast but tire quickly, as in sprinters.'},
  {ofTag:'Behavioral: Sensation and Perception', of:'_____ are stretch receptors within the body of a muscle that mainly detect changes in muscle length', kind:'scenario', q:'When a clinician taps the patellar tendon, the quadriceps is briefly stretched and then contracts. Which receptor detects the initial stretch?', choices:['Muscle spindles','Golgi tendon organs','Pacinian corpuscles','Ruffini endings'], ans:0, exp:'**Muscle spindles** lie parallel to muscle fibers and sense changes in **length**; their Ia afferents synapse directly on motor neurons of the same muscle, producing the monosynaptic knee-jerk. Golgi tendon organs sit in series in the tendon and sense **tension**, inhibiting the muscle.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'The _____ briefly delays the impulse so the atria finish contracting before the ventricles, and is normally the only electrical link between atria and ventricles', kind:'application', q:'An ECG shows a markedly prolonged PR interval with normal P waves and QRS complexes. Conduction through which structure is most likely slowed?', choices:['AV node','SA node','Purkinje fibers','Bundle branches'], ans:0, exp:'The PR interval spans atrial depolarization plus the deliberate **AV nodal delay** that lets the atria finish filling the ventricles. A long PR (first-degree AV block) therefore points to slow AV nodal conduction. Bundle branch or Purkinje delays widen the QRS instead, and SA node problems change the P waves or rate.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'The main function of _____ is to lower blood pressure by promoting renal excretion of sodium and water', kind:'prediction', q:'A patient with heart failure has atria stretched by volume overload. Which hormonal response is expected?', choices:['ANP rises, increasing renal Na⁺ excretion','ANP rises, increasing renal Na⁺ retention','ANP falls, increasing renal Na⁺ excretion','ANP falls, increasing renal Na⁺ retention'], ans:0, exp:'Atrial stretch releases **atrial natriuretic peptide**, which raises GFR, inhibits renin and aldosterone, and promotes **natriuresis** (Na⁺ and water loss), lowering blood volume and pressure. It is the body\'s counterweight to the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'Aldosterone increases _____ reabsorption; water follows, raising blood volume and pressure without much change in blood osmolarity', kind:'prediction', q:'A patient has an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma (Conn syndrome). Which pattern is most likely?', choices:['Hypertension with low plasma K⁺','Hypotension with high plasma K⁺','Hypertension with high plasma K⁺','Hypotension with low plasma Na⁺'], ans:0, exp:'**Aldosterone** acts on principal cells of the distal nephron to increase **Na⁺ reabsorption** (water follows, raising volume and pressure) in exchange for **K⁺ secretion**. Excess aldosterone therefore causes hypertension with hypokalemia, while plasma Na⁺ stays near normal because water is retained with it.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'If blood osmolarity is _____ than normal, the body responds by releasing ADH to retain water', kind:'scenario', q:'A hiker drinks no water for a day, and her plasma osmolarity rises. How do ADH and her urine change?', choices:['ADH rises and urine becomes concentrated','ADH rises and urine becomes dilute','ADH falls and urine becomes concentrated','ADH falls and urine becomes dilute'], ans:0, exp:'Hypothalamic osmoreceptors detect **high plasma osmolarity** and trigger **ADH** release from the posterior pituitary. ADH inserts aquaporin-2 into collecting duct cells, so more water is reabsorbed and a small volume of concentrated urine is made.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'_____ pushes fluid out of a capillary and into the interstitial fluid', kind:'prediction', q:'In left-sided heart failure, blood backs up and pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure rises. What is the expected result?', choices:['Fluid filters into the lung interstitium, causing edema','Fluid is drawn from the alveoli into the capillaries','Plasma oncotic pressure rises enough to stop filtration','Lymph flow out of the lungs stops completely'], ans:0, exp:'**Capillary hydrostatic pressure** pushes fluid out of capillaries; oncotic pressure from albumin pulls it back. When hydrostatic pressure rises, net filtration exceeds lymphatic drainage and fluid collects in the interstitium and alveoli: **pulmonary edema**.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'_____, generated mainly by plasma albumin, draws fluid back into the capillary', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient with nephrotic syndrome loses large amounts of albumin in the urine and develops generalized edema. What is the main cause of the edema?', choices:['Reduced plasma oncotic pressure','Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure','Reduced interstitial hydrostatic pressure','Increased plasma oncotic pressure'], ans:0, exp:'**Albumin** generates most of the plasma **oncotic pressure** that draws fluid back into capillaries. Losing albumin weakens that pull, so more fluid stays in the tissues. Liver failure causes edema the same way, by reducing albumin synthesis.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'The binding of oxygen to hemoglobin is _____', kind:'application', q:'Hemoglobin binds its first O₂ weakly, but each O₂ that binds raises the affinity of the remaining hemes. What feature of the O₂ dissociation curve results?', choices:['A sigmoidal shape','A hyperbolic shape','A linear shape','A flat, saturated shape'], ans:0, exp:'**Cooperative binding** (T state shifting to R state) gives hemoglobin a **sigmoidal** curve, so it loads O₂ nearly fully in the lungs yet unloads a lot over the steep middle range of tissue PO₂. Single-heme myoglobin cannot cooperate and gives a hyperbola.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'A low blood pH _____ hemoglobin\'s affinity for O₂', kind:'prediction', q:'During strenuous exercise, the pH of blood in active muscle falls to about 7.2. How does this affect O₂ delivery?', choices:['The curve shifts right and more O₂ is unloaded','The curve shifts left and more O₂ is unloaded','The curve shifts right and less O₂ is unloaded','The curve shifts left and less O₂ is unloaded'], ans:0, exp:'In the **Bohr effect**, H⁺ binds hemoglobin and stabilizes the low-affinity T state, so **lower pH lowers O₂ affinity**: the curve shifts right and more O₂ is released where it is needed. Mnemonic for right shifts, "CADET face right": CO₂, Acid, 2,3-DPG (BPG), Exercise, Temperature.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'When the body needs more O₂ delivered to tissues (e.g., at high altitude or in chronic anemia), RBC 2,3-BPG levels _____', kind:'scenario', q:'A climber spends two weeks at 4,500 m. Which red blood cell adaptation helps deliver more O₂ to her tissues?', choices:['More 2,3-BPG, shifting the curve right','Less 2,3-BPG, shifting the curve left','More 2,3-BPG, shifting the curve left','Less 2,3-BPG, shifting the curve right'], ans:0, exp:'Chronic hypoxia **raises RBC 2,3-BPG**, which binds deoxyhemoglobin and stabilizes the T state, lowering O₂ affinity and shifting the curve **right** so more O₂ is unloaded in tissues. Over weeks, erythropoietin also raises the red cell mass.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'Fetal hemoglobin (α₂γ₂) has γ subunits in place of β subunits, so _____ binds it less strongly', kind:'application', q:'O₂ moves from maternal hemoglobin to fetal hemoglobin across the placenta. Which feature of HbF makes this transfer possible?', choices:['Its γ chains bind 2,3-BPG weakly, raising O₂ affinity','Its γ chains bind 2,3-BPG strongly, raising O₂ affinity','Its γ chains bind 2,3-BPG weakly, lowering O₂ affinity','Its single heme group makes O₂ binding non-cooperative'], ans:0, exp:'**2,3-BPG** normally lowers adult hemoglobin\'s O₂ affinity by binding between the β chains. The γ chains of HbF bind **2,3-BPG** poorly, so HbF keeps a higher O₂ affinity and pulls O₂ from maternal blood. HbF still has four hemes and binds cooperatively.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'Fetal hemoglobin has a _____ P50 than adult hemoglobin because of its higher O₂ affinity', kind:'prediction', q:'Compared with the adult hemoglobin dissociation curve, where does the fetal hemoglobin curve lie?', choices:['Shifted left, with a lower P50','Shifted right, with a higher P50','Shifted left, with a higher P50','Shifted right, with a lower P50'], ans:0, exp:'Higher O₂ affinity means hemoglobin reaches 50% saturation at a lower PO₂, so HbF has a **lower P50** and a **left-shifted** curve. A left shift and a lower P50 always go together; "shifted left with a higher P50" is self-contradictory.'},
  {ofTag:'Cardiovascular System', of:'Which expression gives cardiac output (CO)?', kind:'application', q:'A patient has a heart rate of 80 beats/min, an end-diastolic volume of 120 mL and an end-systolic volume of 50 mL. What is the cardiac output?', choices:['5.6 L/min','9.6 L/min','4.0 L/min','2.8 L/min'], ans:0, exp:'Stroke volume = EDV − ESV = 120 − 50 = 70 mL, and **CO = HR × SV** = 80 × 70 mL = 5,600 mL/min = 5.6 L/min. Multiplying heart rate by EDV (9.6) or ESV (4.0) alone is the common error.'},
  {ofTag:'Respiratory System: Pathway', of:'Surfactant _____ surface tension in the alveoli', kind:'scenario', q:'An infant born at 28 weeks develops respiratory distress, and the alveoli collapse at the end of each breath. Deficiency of which substance is responsible?', choices:['Surfactant from type II pneumocytes','Mucus from goblet cells','Elastin in the alveolar walls','Cilia on the airway epithelium'], ans:0, exp:'**Surfactant**, made by type II pneumocytes from late in gestation, **lowers surface tension** in the alveoli so small alveoli do not collapse at end-expiration (Laplace\'s law). Premature infants lack enough of it, causing neonatal respiratory distress syndrome; maternal glucocorticoids speed its production.'},
  {ofTag:'Respiratory System: Bicarbonate Buffer', of:'If blood pH falls too low, the respiratory rate will _____', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient in diabetic ketoacidosis has an arterial pH of 7.15. Which compensatory breathing change is expected?', choices:['Deep, rapid breathing that lowers PaCO₂','Slow, shallow breathing that raises PaCO₂','Breath-holding that raises PaCO₂','No change until the kidneys compensate'], ans:0, exp:'Low pH stimulates the chemoreceptors and **increases ventilation**, blowing off CO₂ and shifting CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ to the left, which lowers H⁺. This **Kussmaul breathing** is respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis; renal compensation takes days.'},
  {ofTag:'Respiratory System', of:'_____ is the volume of air that remains in the lungs after a maximal exhalation', kind:'except', q:'Which of the following can NOT be measured directly with a simple spirometer?', choices:['Residual volume','Tidal volume','Expiratory reserve volume','Vital capacity'], ans:0, exp:'A spirometer measures air moving in and out, so it cannot measure the **residual volume** that stays in the lungs even after maximal exhalation; RV (and therefore TLC and FRC) needs helium dilution or body plethysmography. Tidal volume, ERV and vital capacity are all exhaled volumes.'},
  {ofTag:'Kidney', of:'The _____ is the site of bulk reabsorption of glucose, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, salt and water', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient with type 2 diabetes starts an SGLT2 inhibitor and develops glucosuria. Transport in which nephron segment is being blocked?', choices:['Proximal convoluted tubule','Thick ascending limb','Distal convoluted tubule','Collecting duct'], ans:0, exp:'Nearly all filtered glucose is reclaimed in the **proximal convoluted tubule**, mostly by **SGLT2** (Na⁺–glucose cotransport). Blocking it lets glucose spill into the urine, lowering blood glucose. Glucose normally appears in urine only when filtered load exceeds the PCT transport maximum.'},
  {ofTag:'Kidney', of:'The _____ is permeable to water but not to salt', kind:'prediction', q:'As filtrate flows down the descending limb of the loop of Henle into the hypertonic medulla, what happens to its osmolarity?', choices:['It rises, because water leaves while salt stays','It falls, because salt leaves while water stays','It rises, because salt is actively pumped in','It is unchanged, because water and salt leave together'], ans:0, exp:'The **descending limb** is permeable to water but not to salt, so water moves out into the hypertonic medullary interstitium and the filtrate becomes more concentrated toward the tip of the loop. The ascending limb does the reverse, pumping out salt while staying impermeable to water.'},
  {ofTag:'Kidney', of:'The thick ascending limb is called the diluting segment because it actively reabsorbs _____ while remaining impermeable to water', kind:'scenario', q:'Furosemide blocks the Na⁺–K⁺–2Cl⁻ cotransporter in the thick ascending limb. Why does this produce a large diuresis?', choices:['The medullary gradient collapses, so less water is reabsorbed','Glucose stays in the filtrate and holds water osmotically','ADH can no longer bind its receptor in the collecting duct','Aldosterone secretion stops and Na⁺ is lost'], ans:0, exp:'The thick ascending limb **actively reabsorbs salt** while staying impermeable to water, which dilutes the filtrate and builds the hypertonic medullary gradient. Loop diuretics block this, so the gradient washes out and the collecting duct cannot pull water back, even with ADH present.'},
  {ofTag:'Kidney', of:'The _____ responds to aldosterone and, like the PCT, reabsorbs salt and secretes waste products', kind:'prediction', q:'Spironolactone blocks the aldosterone receptor. In which region of the nephron is Na⁺ reabsorption most directly reduced?', choices:['The distal tubule and collecting duct','The proximal convoluted tubule','The descending limb of the loop','The glomerulus and Bowman capsule'], ans:0, exp:'**Aldosterone** acts on the **distal nephron** (late DCT and collecting duct), increasing ENaC and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity to reabsorb Na⁺ and secrete K⁺. Blocking it causes mild natriuresis while sparing K⁺, which is why spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic.'},
  {ofTag:'Kidney', of:'The _____ has variable water permeability, allowing reabsorption of the right amount of water for the body\'s needs', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient with nephrogenic diabetes insipidus has collecting duct cells that do not respond to ADH. What kind of urine does the patient produce?', choices:['Large volumes of dilute urine','Small volumes of concentrated urine','Large volumes of concentrated urine','Small volumes of dilute urine'], ans:0, exp:'The **collecting duct**\'s water permeability is set by ADH-inserted aquaporin-2 channels. Without an ADH response, the duct stays nearly impermeable, so the dilute fluid leaving the thick ascending limb passes through unchanged: high-volume, dilute urine and constant thirst.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'Type _____ diabetes is a chronic condition in which autoimmune destruction of β cells leaves the pancreas producing little or no insulin', kind:'scenario', q:'A 10-year-old presents with weight loss and polyuria; his C-peptide is very low and he has antibodies against islet antigens. Which diagnosis fits?', choices:['Type 1 diabetes mellitus','Type 2 diabetes mellitus','Central diabetes insipidus','Cushing syndrome'], ans:0, exp:'**Type 1 diabetes** is autoimmune destruction of β cells, so insulin and its co-secreted C-peptide are both low and islet autoantibodies are present. Type 2 diabetes begins with insulin resistance and normal or high C-peptide. Diabetes insipidus causes polyuria without hyperglycemia.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'Type _____ diabetes is a chronic condition in which target tissues become resistant to insulin', kind:'prediction', q:'Which pattern of fasting insulin and glucose is most typical of early type 2 diabetes?', choices:['High insulin with high glucose','Low insulin with high glucose','High insulin with low glucose','Low insulin with low glucose'], ans:0, exp:'In **type 2 diabetes** target tissues are **insulin-resistant**, so β cells compensate by secreting more insulin; glucose still rises because the insulin is less effective. Low insulin with high glucose is the type 1 pattern (or late type 2 once β cells fail), and high insulin with low glucose suggests an insulinoma.'},
  {ofTag:'Biochemistry: Metabolism', of:'Released by pancreatic α cells, _____ causes the liver to break down stored glycogen into glucose', kind:'prediction', q:'Several hours after a meal, blood glucose falls. Which hepatic response does the main pancreatic α-cell hormone trigger?', choices:['Glycogen breakdown via cAMP and protein kinase A','Glycogen synthesis via insulin receptor signaling','Glucose uptake via GLUT4 insertion','Fatty acid synthesis via acetyl-CoA carboxylase'], ans:0, exp:'Falling glucose releases **glucagon**, which binds a Gs-coupled receptor on hepatocytes, raises cAMP and activates PKA; PKA activates glycogen phosphorylase (via phosphorylase kinase) and inhibits glycogen synthase, releasing glucose. GLUT4 insertion and lipogenesis are insulin effects.'},
  {ofTag:'Biochemistry: Metabolism', of:'Cortisol increases blood glucose mainly by _____', kind:'except', q:'During prolonged stress, cortisol raises blood glucose by all of the following mechanisms EXCEPT:', choices:['Stimulating insulin secretion from β cells','Inducing gluconeogenic enzymes in the liver','Promoting muscle protein breakdown for substrate','Reducing glucose uptake by peripheral tissues'], ans:0, exp:'**Cortisol** raises glucose mainly by **stimulating gluconeogenesis**: it induces enzymes such as PEPCK, supplies amino acids by breaking down muscle protein, and opposes insulin action in peripheral tissues. Stimulating insulin release would lower glucose, so it is not one of cortisol\'s glucose-raising actions.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'_____ raises blood Ca²⁺ partly by stimulating bone resorption', kind:'scenario', q:'After thyroid surgery, a patient develops perioral tingling and muscle spasms because the parathyroid glands were removed. Which lab pattern is expected?', choices:['Low calcium and high phosphate','High calcium and low phosphate','Low calcium and low phosphate','High calcium and high phosphate'], ans:0, exp:'**Parathyroid hormone** raises blood Ca²⁺ by stimulating bone resorption, renal Ca²⁺ reabsorption and calcitriol synthesis, and it lowers phosphate by blocking its renal reabsorption. Without PTH, calcium falls (causing tetany) and phosphate rises.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'_____ stimulates the thyroid to produce thyroxine (T₄) and triiodothyronine (T₃), which raise metabolic rate', kind:'prediction', q:'A patient has primary hypothyroidism caused by Hashimoto thyroiditis. What are the expected TSH and free T₄ levels?', choices:['High TSH, low T₄','Low TSH, low T₄','High TSH, high T₄','Low TSH, high T₄'], ans:0, exp:'**TSH** drives T₄/T₃ production, and T₄/T₃ feed back to suppress TRH and TSH. When the thyroid itself fails, T₄ falls and the loss of negative feedback makes TSH rise. Low TSH with low T₄ would point to a pituitary (secondary) cause.'},
  {ofTag:'Biochemistry: Metabolism', of:'_____ stimulates the adrenal cortex to produce and release cortisol', kind:'prediction', q:'A patient takes high-dose prednisone, a synthetic glucocorticoid, for several months. What happens to ACTH and the adrenal cortex?', choices:['ACTH falls and the adrenal cortex atrophies','ACTH rises and the adrenal cortex enlarges','ACTH falls and the adrenal cortex enlarges','ACTH rises and the adrenal cortex atrophies'], ans:0, exp:'Exogenous glucocorticoid exerts **negative feedback** on CRH and **ACTH**. Without ACTH stimulation, the cortisol-producing zones of the adrenal cortex atrophy, which is why stopping long-term steroids abruptly can cause adrenal insufficiency. ACTH is the trophic hormone for the cortex.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'The _____ releases oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH)', kind:'scenario', q:'After a head injury damages the posterior pituitary, a patient produces large volumes of dilute urine. Which hormone is deficient?', choices:['ADH','Aldosterone','Oxytocin','ACTH'], ans:0, exp:'The **posterior pituitary** stores and releases **ADH** and oxytocin, both made in the hypothalamus. Losing ADH causes central diabetes insipidus: the collecting ducts stay impermeable to water. Oxytocin is also lost, but that does not affect urine; ACTH comes from the anterior pituitary.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'Hormones derived from cholesterol, such as cortisol and estrogen, are _____ hormones', kind:'prediction', q:'How does cortisol, a cholesterol-derived hormone, typically act on its target cells?', choices:['It enters the cell and binds a receptor that alters transcription','It binds a surface G protein-coupled receptor and raises cAMP','It binds a receptor tyrosine kinase that then autophosphorylates','It opens a ligand-gated ion channel in the plasma membrane'], ans:0, exp:'**Steroid hormones** are lipophilic, so they diffuse through the plasma membrane and bind **intracellular receptors** that act as transcription factors. Their effects are slower but longer-lasting than those of peptide hormones, which must bind surface receptors and use second messengers.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'cAMP signaling is turned off when _____ breaks the ring of cAMP and converts it to AMP', kind:'prediction', q:'Caffeine inhibits cAMP phosphodiesterase. What happens in a liver cell stimulated by glucagon?', choices:['cAMP stays elevated longer, prolonging PKA activity','cAMP falls faster, shortening PKA activity','cAMP synthesis by adenylyl cyclase stops','The glucagon receptor is internalized faster'], ans:0, exp:'**Phosphodiesterase** ends cAMP signaling by hydrolyzing cAMP to AMP. Blocking it lets cAMP accumulate, so protein kinase A stays active longer and the glucagon response (glycogen breakdown) is amplified. Adenylyl cyclase, which makes cAMP, is not affected.'},
  {ofTag:'Endocrine', of:'After an activated Gα subunit has regulated its target enzyme, how is G protein signaling normally switched off?', kind:'scenario', q:'Cholera toxin modifies Gsα so that it can no longer hydrolyze its bound GTP. What happens in intestinal epithelial cells?', choices:['Adenylyl cyclase stays on and cAMP remains high','Adenylyl cyclase is inhibited and cAMP falls','Gsα stays bound to GDP and remains inactive','The receptor can no longer bind its ligand'], ans:0, exp:'Gα normally switches itself off by **hydrolyzing GTP to GDP** and rejoining Gβγ. When it cannot, Gsα stays active and keeps adenylyl cyclase on, so cAMP stays high, CFTR keeps secreting Cl⁻, and water follows, causing massive watery diarrhea.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Innate', of:'_____ displays exogenous antigen to CD4⁺ helper T cells', kind:'prediction', q:'A child has a mutation that prevents MHC class II expression. Which immune function is most impaired?', choices:['Activation of CD4⁺ helper T cells','Killing of tumor cells by NK cells','Activation of the alternative complement pathway','Presentation of viral peptides to CD8⁺ T cells'], ans:0, exp:'**MHC II**, found on professional antigen-presenting cells, shows peptides from engulfed (exogenous) antigens to **CD4⁺ helper T cells**. Without it, helper T cells cannot be activated, so B cell and other adaptive responses also fail. CD8⁺ T cells use MHC I, and NK cells and complement are innate.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Innate', of:'_____ displays endogenous antigen to cytotoxic CD8⁺ T cells', kind:'application', q:'A virus-infected hepatocyte displays fragments of viral proteins made in its own cytosol. Which molecule presents them, and to which cell?', choices:['MHC I, to CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells','MHC II, to CD4⁺ helper T cells','MHC I, to CD4⁺ helper T cells','MHC II, to CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells'], ans:0, exp:'Proteins made inside a cell (**endogenous**, such as viral proteins) are processed by the proteasome and loaded onto **MHC I**, found on all nucleated cells, for recognition by **CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells**. Rule of 8: MHC I × CD8 = 8, and MHC II × CD4 = 8.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Innate', of:'_____ are innate lymphocytes that kill cancer cells or virus-infected cells without prior sensitization, often by detecting reduced MHC I expression', kind:'scenario', q:'A tumor escapes cytotoxic T cells by shutting down MHC class I expression. Which immune cell is best placed to kill it?', choices:['Natural killer cells','Helper T cells','Plasma cells','Regulatory T cells'], ans:0, exp:'**Natural killer cells** check for MHC I as a "self" signal; when a cell has lost MHC I (missing self), the inhibitory signal disappears and the NK cell kills it. This complements cytotoxic T cells, which need MHC I to see antigen at all.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Adaptive', of:'_____ is the high rate of mutation in the antigen-binding (variable) region of antibody genes in activated B cells', kind:'application', q:'Antibodies against a vaccine antigen bind more tightly after a booster dose than after the first dose. Which process in germinal-center B cells produces this affinity maturation?', choices:['Somatic hypermutation of variable-region genes','Class switching of constant-region genes','VDJ recombination in the bone marrow','Negative selection in the thymus'], ans:0, exp:'**Somatic hypermutation** introduces point mutations into antibody **variable-region** genes of activated B cells; those with higher-affinity receptors are selected, so affinity rises over repeated exposures. Class switching changes the isotype (e.g., IgM to IgG) but not the antigen-binding site.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Adaptive', of:'_____ is the coating of a pathogen with antibodies or complement (C3b) to mark it for phagocytosis', kind:'application', q:'Patients without a spleen are vulnerable to encapsulated bacteria, partly because splenic macrophages normally remove bacteria coated with IgG and C3b. What is this coating process called?', choices:['Opsonization','Agglutination','Neutralization','Degranulation'], ans:0, exp:'**Opsonization** coats a microbe with IgG or C3b so phagocytes, which have receptors for these molecules, can grab and engulf it. Capsules resist phagocytosis unless opsonized, which is why asplenic patients are vaccinated against pneumococcus, meningococcus and Hib. Agglutination is clumping, and neutralization blocks binding.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Adaptive', of:'Vaccination induces _____, in which the person\'s own B cells are activated to make antibodies and memory cells', kind:'scenario', q:'A nurse completes the hepatitis B vaccine series and later has anti-HBs antibodies and memory B cells. What type of immunity is this?', choices:['Artificial active immunity','Artificial passive immunity','Natural passive immunity','Innate immunity'], ans:0, exp:'Vaccination makes the person\'s **own** B cells produce antibodies and memory cells: **active immunity**, termed artificial because exposure was deliberate. Passive immunity transfers ready-made antibodies (immune globulin or maternal IgG) and leaves no memory.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Adaptive', of:'The transfer of pre-formed antibodies to an individual produces _____', kind:'prediction', q:'After a needlestick from a hepatitis B-positive patient, an unvaccinated worker receives hepatitis B immune globulin. Which statement describes this protection?', choices:['Immediate but short-lived, without memory cells','Delayed but long-lived, with memory cells','Immediate and lifelong, with memory cells','Delayed and short-lived, without memory cells'], ans:0, exp:'**Passive immunity** gives ready-made antibodies that work at once but are degraded within weeks to months, and no memory cells form because the recipient\'s B cells were never activated. That is why the vaccine is usually given at the same time to build active immunity.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Adaptive', of:'During T-cell maturation in the thymus, negative selection _____', kind:'prediction', q:'If negative selection in the thymus failed, what would most likely result?', choices:['Self-reactive T cells would escape and cause autoimmunity','T cells unable to recognize self-MHC would accumulate','B cells would fail to switch antibody class','NK cells would attack all MHC I-bearing cells'], ans:0, exp:'**Negative selection** deletes thymocytes that bind self-peptide–MHC too strongly, enforcing central tolerance. Without it, self-reactive T cells reach the periphery and can cause autoimmune disease (e.g., AIRE deficiency). Removing T cells that cannot bind self-MHC is the job of **positive** selection.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Adaptive', of:'_____ activate cytotoxic T cells and macrophages to attack infected cells, and stimulate B cells to secrete antibodies', kind:'scenario', q:'In advanced HIV infection, CD4⁺ T-cell counts fall. Why are both antibody responses and cytotoxic T-cell responses weakened?', choices:['Helper T cells normally activate B cells and cytotoxic T cells','Helper T cells normally present antigen on MHC I molecules','Helper T cells normally secrete most circulating antibody','Helper T cells normally kill virus-infected cells directly'], ans:0, exp:'CD4⁺ **helper T cells** coordinate adaptive immunity: their cytokines and CD40L drive B cell class switching and antibody production and help activate CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells and macrophages. Losing them cripples both arms, which leads to opportunistic infections in AIDS. Plasma cells, not helper T cells, secrete antibody.'},
  {ofTag:'Immune System: Adaptive', of:'Activated B cells differentiate into _____ that secrete large amounts of antibody', kind:'scenario', q:'Multiple myeloma is a cancer in which one clone of cells makes huge amounts of a single monoclonal antibody. Which cell type is malignant?', choices:['Plasma cells','Helper T cells','Memory T cells','Mast cells'], ans:0, exp:'**Plasma cells** are terminally differentiated B cells packed with rough ER for secreting antibody. A malignant plasma cell clone produces one monoclonal immunoglobulin (an M spike), as in multiple myeloma. T cells and mast cells do not secrete antibodies.'},
  {ofTag:'Digestion: Small Intestine', of:'Released when acidic chyme enters the duodenum, _____ stimulates the pancreas to secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid', kind:'prediction', q:'Acidic chyme from the stomach enters the duodenum. Which hormonal response protects the intestinal lining?', choices:['Secretin triggers pancreatic bicarbonate secretion','Gastrin triggers gastric bicarbonate secretion','CCK triggers gallbladder bicarbonate secretion','Secretin triggers more acid secretion by parietal cells'], ans:0, exp:'Duodenal S cells sense low pH and release **secretin**, which makes pancreatic duct cells secrete **bicarbonate-rich fluid** to neutralize the acid; secretin also inhibits gastric acid secretion. CCK mainly drives enzyme secretion and gallbladder contraction, and gastrin increases acid.'},
  {ofTag:'Digestion: Small Intestine', of:'_____ stimulates bile release from the gallbladder and pancreatic enzyme secretion, and promotes satiety', kind:'scenario', q:'A patient with gallstones has right upper quadrant pain about 30 minutes after a fatty meal. Which hormone, released by fat in the duodenum, causes the gallbladder to contract?', choices:['CCK','Secretin','Gastrin','GIP'], ans:0, exp:'Fat and protein in the duodenum stimulate I cells to release **cholecystokinin (CCK)**, which contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion; contraction against a stone causes the pain. Secretin mainly stimulates bicarbonate, and gastrin mainly stimulates acid.'},
  {ofTag:'Digestion: Small Intestine', of:'_____ is a duodenal brush-border enzyme that converts trypsinogen into trypsin', kind:'prediction', q:'An infant with congenital enteropeptidase deficiency has severe protein malabsorption. Why are nearly all pancreatic proteases affected, not just trypsin?', choices:['Trypsin, once activated, activates the other zymogens','Enteropeptidase directly activates each zymogen in turn','Pepsin needs enteropeptidase before it can activate trypsin','Bile salts need trypsin before they can emulsify protein'], ans:0, exp:'**Enteropeptidase** on the duodenal brush border converts trypsinogen to **trypsin**, which then activates chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, procarboxypeptidase and more trypsinogen. Without that first step the whole protease cascade stays inactive. Keeping these enzymes as zymogens until they reach the duodenum protects the pancreas.'},
];
